/* Field cases — batch 3 */
window.CASES=window.CASES||[];
window.CASES.push(
{ id:"fcp23", kind:"case", title:"Greenhouse sensor nodes dying in two weeks", level:1, domains:["p23"],
  context:"You built twelve battery-powered sensor nodes for a customer's greenhouse. Each one is a cheap ESP32 dev board, a BME280 on I2C, and a protected 18650 cell (2,500 mAh) wired into the board's 5V/VIN pin. The firmware wakes every 10 minutes, reads the sensor, publishes over MQTT with TLS on port 8883, and goes back to deep sleep. Your quote promised battery swaps twice a year, based on duty-cycle math that gave about 140 days per charge. The first node dropped off the broker after 11 days, and four more followed by day 12. Each swap visit costs you about an hour of unbilled labor, so the contract loses money until this is fixed. You have one node on the bench with an INA219 in the battery lead.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"INA219 log, bench node, one wake cycle (battery lead, cell at 3.92 V)",
     text:"time_s   current_mA   note\n0.00     5.61         deep sleep\n0.40     5.62         deep sleep\n0.81     48.3         RTC timer wake, boot\n1.10     121.4        Wi-Fi associating\n1.55     238.7        TX burst (peak)\n2.02     142.6        TLS handshake to broker\n2.60     151.9        MQTT publish, QoS 1\n3.05     139.0        PUBACK received\n3.21     5.64         esp_deep_sleep_start()\n...      5.6x         steady until next wake\nSummary over 600.0 s: awake 2.40 s at mean 150 mA; asleep 597.6 s at mean 5.62 mA"},
    {label:"Board power path, datasheet excerpts and one bench test",
     text:"Board: ESP32-WROOM-32 dev board clone (30-pin), as bought, unmodified\nPower path: 18650 + DW01 protection -> VIN (5V pin) -> U2 AMS1117-3.3 -> 3V3 rail\n3V3 rail loads: ESP32 module, BME280 breakout, CP2102 USB-UART bridge,\n                D1 red power LED + 1 kOhm resistor (always on)\nAMS1117-3.3: quiescent current 5 mA typ (10 mA max); dropout 1.1 V typ\nRed LED: Vf about 2.0 V\nESP32 deep sleep (RTC timer wake only): about 10 uA\nBME280 sleep mode: 0.1 uA\nMCP1700-3302E: quiescent 1.6 uA, dropout 178 mV at 250 mA, 250 mA max\nESP32 brownout detector: resets the chip when the 3V3 rail sags below its threshold\nBench test: D1 desoldered -> sleep current falls from 5.62 mA to 4.33 mA"},
    {label:"Dashboard export, customer note, cell test and serial console",
     text:"node   days_alive  last_vbat  last_rssi  awake_s(avg)\ngh-03  11.2        3.57 V     -64 dBm    2.38\ngh-07  11.0        3.60 V     -79 dBm    2.41\ngh-09  11.6        3.55 V     -61 dBm    2.36\ngh-11  11.9        3.58 V     -66 dBm    2.44\ngh-12  11.4        3.56 V     -70 dBm    2.39\nCustomer note: \"gh-07 has the weakest Wi-Fi. Is the router killing the batteries?\"\nBench: gh-07 cell, capacity test 4.20 -> 3.00 V at 0.5 A: 2,430 mAh\nSerial console, bench node fed from a cell at 3.58 V:\nBrownout detector was triggered\nets Jul 29 2019 12:21:46\nrst:0xf (BROWNOUT_RST),boot:0x13 (SPI_FAST_FLASH_BOOT)\n(repeats every few seconds; no MQTT publish completes)"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp23q1", q:"The node sleeps at 5.62 mA instead of the 60 uA the quote assumed. Where does that current go?",
     options:["The ESP32 never really enters deep sleep, because the firmware does not call esp_deep_sleep_start()","The board, not the chip: about 1.3 mA through the always-on power LED and about 4.3 mA of AMS1117 quiescent current, while the ESP32 itself draws about 10 uA","The BME280 is left in normal mode between readings and draws the extra current","Weak Wi-Fi keeps the radio partly on during deep sleep"],
     answer:1, why:"Desoldering the LED removed 1.29 mA, which matches (3.3 V - 2.0 V) / 1 kOhm. The 4.33 mA left over matches the AMS1117's milliamp-class quiescent current. The log shows esp_deep_sleep_start() and a drop from 139 mA to 5.64 mA; an awake ESP32 would draw tens of milliamps. The BME280 sleeps at 0.1 uA, and the radio is off in deep sleep whatever the RSSI."},
    {id:"fcp23q2", q:"Using the measured numbers, how long should 2,000 mAh (80% of the cell) last?",
     options:["About 140 days, as quoted: the node sleeps 99.6% of the time, so the sleep current hardly matters","About 8 hours, because the 238.7 mA transmit peak sets the drain","It can't be estimated without knowing each node's Wi-Fi signal strength","About 13 days: the average is (150 x 2.4 + 5.62 x 597.6) / 600, about 6.2 mA, and 2,000 / 6.2 is about 323 hours"],
     answer:3, why:"A duty-cycle average weights each current by how long it flows. Per cycle the node uses 360 mA·s awake and 3,358 mA·s asleep, so sleep is about 90% of the charge. The quote's 140 days assumed a 60 uA floor. Peak current matters for supply design, not for the average. The nodes actually died at 11–12 days, earlier still, which the next question explains."},
    {id:"fcp23q3", q:"Why did the nodes go silent with their cells at 3.55–3.60 V, far above the 3.0 V cutoff?",
     options:["The DW01 protection chip disconnected the cell","The cells are counterfeit and hold far less than 2,500 mAh","The AMS1117 needs about 1.1 V of headroom, so a 3.6 V cell gives a rail near 2.5 V. The 240 mA transmit burst then pulls it below the brownout threshold, and the node reboots in a loop with about a third of its charge unused","Below 3.6 V the BME280 stops answering and the firmware hangs waiting for it"],
     answer:2, why:"The console shows BROWNOUT_RST loops at 3.58 V, which is a supply-dip signature. A DW01 cuts out near 2.4 V, not 3.6 V. The cell tests at 2,430 mAh, so it is genuine. The node runs out of regulator headroom long before it runs out of charge."},
    {id:"fcp23q4", q:"After you fit a low-quiescent LDO and remove the LED, sleep current measures 14 uA. What is now the biggest lever on battery life?",
     options:["Awake time. 150 mA for 2.4 s is now about 98% of the charge per cycle, so halving it (static IP, Wi-Fi channel and BSSID saved in RTC memory, TLS session resumption) nearly doubles battery life","Cutting sleep current further, from 14 uA to 10 uA","Switching MQTT from TLS on 8883 to plaintext 1883 to save the handshake","Sampling every 5 minutes instead of 10"],
     answer:0, why:"Per cycle, awake is 360 mA·s and sleep is now 0.014 x 597.6 = 8.4 mA·s, so the awake share is about 98%. Shaving microamps off sleep changes almost nothing. Plaintext MQTT belongs only on a lab network, and sampling twice as often roughly halves battery life."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the diagnosis and fix for the customer's nodes. Show with arithmetic where the charge goes and explain why the nodes die above 3.5 V. Specify the hardware and firmware changes, predict the new battery life, and say how you will verify it on the bench before the next site visit.",
    rubric:["Computes the measured average (about 6.2 mA from 150 mA x 2.4 s plus 5.62 mA x 597.6 s over 600 s) and shows that sleep, not the awake burst, uses about 90% of the charge.","Attributes the 5.62 mA sleep floor to the board: about 1.3 mA through the power LED (confirmed by the desolder test) and about 4.3 mA of AMS1117 quiescent current. Rules out the ESP32 and the BME280 with their datasheet figures.","Explains the early death near 3.57 V: the AMS1117's roughly 1.1 V dropout leaves the rail near 2.5 V, and transmit peaks trip the brownout detector (BROWNOUT_RST) with roughly 30% of the charge unused.","Specifies the fix: a low-quiescent, low-dropout regulator (MCP1700-class with bulk capacitance sized for transmit peaks, or a low-Iq buck-boost) feeding 3V3, the LED removed or a battery-oriented board used, and MQTT kept on TLS.","Predicts the new life with arithmetic (sleep in the tens of microamps, average about 0.61–0.66 mA, about 125–135 days) and names awake time as the next lever.","Dismisses the Wi-Fi and counterfeit-cell theories with evidence (2.36–2.44 s awake on every node including gh-07; 2,430 mAh capacity test) and verifies with an INA219 cycle log before redeploying."],
    reference:"The quote assumed a 60 uA sleep floor, but the boards sleep at 5.62 mA. Per 600 s cycle a node uses 150 mA x 2.4 s = 360 mA·s awake and 5.62 mA x 597.6 s = 3,358 mA·s asleep, an average of 6.2 mA. Sleep is 90% of the charge, so 2,000 mAh lasts about 323 hours, roughly 13 days. The ESP32 (about 10 uA asleep) and the BME280 (0.1 uA) aren't the problem; the board is. Desoldering the power LED removed 1.29 mA, which matches (3.3 - 2.0 V) / 1 kOhm, and the remaining 4.3 mA is the AMS1117's own quiescent current. The nodes died at 11–12 days instead of 13 because the AMS1117 needs about 1.1 V of headroom. With the cell near 3.6 V the rail sits around 2.5 V, the 240 mA transmit burst pulls it below the brownout threshold, and the console shows BROWNOUT_RST loops with a third of the charge still in the cell. gh-07's -79 dBm signal is a red herring (its awake time matches the others), and the cells test at 2,430 mAh. Fix: feed 3V3 from a low-quiescent LDO (MCP1700-class with bulk capacitance for transmit peaks, or a low-Iq buck-boost), remove the LED, and keep TLS. At about 15 uA asleep the average falls to about 0.61 mA, roughly 135 days. Awake time is then 98% of the budget, so it is the next lever. Verify with an INA219 log first.",
    alternatives:["Replace the dev boards with a purpose-built low-power ESP32 board (low-Iq regulator, no always-on LED, USB bridge powered only from USB). This gets the same result with less rework, but you still have to measure its sleep current instead of trusting the listing.","Power the module's 3V3 pin directly from a LiFePO4 cell (3.2 V nominal) and skip the regulator. That removes the quiescent and dropout losses, but it needs a LiFePO4 charger and a check that the cell's full-charge voltage stays within the ESP32's 3.6 V maximum.","Shortening awake time alone (static IP, QoS 0) is only partly right. It cuts the 360 mA·s awake share but leaves the 3,358 mA·s sleep floor, so life only goes from about 13 to about 14 days."] },
  hints:["Multiply each current by how long it flows during one 600-second cycle. Which phase uses most of the charge?","The ESP32 sleeps at about 10 uA. What else on the board is powered all the time, and what did desoldering the LED tell you?","The AMS1117 burns about 4.3 mA itself and the LED 1.3 mA, and its 1.1 V dropout browns the node out near 3.6 V. Fit a low-Iq LDO, drop the LED, then redo the math."] },

{ id:"fcp24", kind:"case", title:"A new backhaul fiber that passes at 1310 and fails at 1550", level:1, domains:["p24"],
  context:"You're the lead tech on a new 3.2 km underground single-mode link (G.652.D, 144-count loose tube, 0.60 in OD) from HUT-A to HUT-B. Pigtails are spliced on at both patch panels (LC/UPC), with two mid-span splice points at HH-07 and HH-19. The carrier's engineer plans to light it with a CWDM mux using channels from 1471 to 1611 nm, and turn-up is Thursday. Your acceptance package came back rejected: fiber 7 fails insertion loss at 1550 nm while 1310 nm passes. The engineer wants the fault located, fixed and re-tested, and the as-built updated, before Wednesday's dispatch. The carrier's acceptance spec is the TIA-568.3 budget, plus at most 0.15 dB per splice (bidirectional average), at most 0.50 dB per connector, and UPC reflectance of -50 dB or better.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"OLTS results, fiber 7 (one-cord reference, both directions)",
     text:"Link: HUT-A FDP-1 port 7 <-> HUT-B FDP-1 port 7    Length (OTDR): 3.20 km\nReference: one-cord method (TIA-526-7-A), LC/UPC reference-grade test cords\nWavelength   A->B     B->A     Average   TIA max   Result\n1310 nm      2.71 dB  2.66 dB  2.69 dB   4.3 dB    PASS\n1550 nm      4.93 dB  4.88 dB  4.91 dB   4.3 dB    FAIL\nBudget basis: fiber 3.2 km x 0.5 dB/km = 1.6 dB; 4 splices x 0.3 = 1.2 dB;\n              2 connector pairs x 0.75 = 1.5 dB; maximum 4.3 dB\nRest of tube 1 (fibers 1-12): 1550 nm averages 3.88-5.40 dB, 7 of 12 FAIL;\n              1310 nm averages 2.21-2.74 dB, all PASS"},
    {label:"OTDR event table, fiber 7, A->B (500 m launch and receive cords, IOR 1.4680)",
     text:"Event  Dist (m)  Type              Loss 1310  Loss 1550  Refl (dB)\n1      500.0     Reflective        0.28       0.30       -55.2\n2      512.4     Non-reflective    0.03       0.04       --\n3      1610.8    Non-reflective    0.04       0.05       --\n4      2347.1    Non-reflective    0.40       2.96       --\n5      2910.6    Non-reflective   -0.07      -0.06       --\n6      3687.6    Non-reflective    0.04       0.05       --\n7      3700.0    Reflective        0.74       0.78       -41.5\n8      4200.3    End (reflective)  --         --         -14.6\nSection attenuation: 0.34 dB/km at 1310 nm, 0.20 dB/km at 1550 nm"},
    {label:"OTDR B->A (same settings) and as-built excerpt",
     text:"B->A event table, fiber 7\nEvent  Dist (m)  Loss 1310  Loss 1550  Refl (dB)\n1      500.0     0.70       0.74       -41.8   (HUT-B FDP-1 port 7)\n2      512.4     0.05       0.06       --\n3      1289.4    0.17       0.16       --\n4      1852.9    0.44       3.02       --\n5      2589.2    0.06       0.05       --\n6      3687.6    0.03       0.03       --\n7      3700.0    0.27       0.29       -55.0   (HUT-A FDP-1 port 7)\n\nAs-built, cable sheath footage from HUT-A (sequential marks):\nHH-07 SPLICE 3,608 ft | HH-11 HH 4,890 ft | HH-14 HH 6,000 ft (slack 30 ft)\nHH-19 SPLICE 7,830 ft | HUT-B FDP 10,395 ft\nHH-14 note 10/21: lid cracked by a vehicle; box replaced with 17 x 30 in.\n  (only size on the truck); slack re-coiled tighter and zip-tied to fit, lid closes"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp24q1", q:"OTDR event 4 (about 1,847 m into the link) loses 0.40 dB at 1310 nm but 2.96 dB at 1550 nm, and almost the same from the other end. What is it?",
     options:["A bad fusion splice that needs to be redone","A gainer caused by fibers with different mode-field diameters","Water in the buffer tube absorbing light at the water peak","A macrobend: a non-reflective point loss that grows steeply with wavelength, at HH-14 (1,847 m of fiber is about 6,000 ft of sheath), where the slack loop was re-coiled tighter than the bend radius"],
     answer:3, why:"Bend loss grows sharply with wavelength, while splice loss is nearly flat. There is no splice at HH-14 anyway. A gainer is direction-dependent and averages out, but event 4 reads the same from both ends. Water absorption peaks at 1383 nm and is spread along the fiber rather than at one point. With about 1% excess fiber length, 1,847 m of fiber matches 6,000 ft (1,829 m) of sheath, which is HH-14."},
    {id:"fcp24q2", q:"Event 5 (HH-19) reads -0.07 dB from A and +0.17 dB from B at 1310 nm. What do you report?",
     options:["A splice loss of 0.05 dB, the average of the two directions. It passes the 0.15 dB spec and needs no work","A splice that adds 0.07 dB of optical gain","A failed 0.17 dB splice, taking the worse direction. Re-splice it","An OTDR ghost; delete it from the event table"],
     answer:0, why:"A gainer happens when the downstream fiber backscatters more than the upstream one, usually because of a mode-field-diameter difference. One direction reads low and the other high, and the true loss is the average: (-0.07 + 0.17) / 2 = 0.05 dB. A ghost has no loss and sits at a multiple of a strong reflector's distance, and this event is at a documented splice."},
    {id:"fcp24q3", q:"The HUT-B connector measures 0.74 dB with -41.5 dB reflectance. That is under the TIA maximum of 0.75 dB. What do you do?",
     options:["Accept it, because it meets TIA-568.3","Swap in an APC jumper to lower the reflectance","Inspect the jumper and the bulkhead side with a probe, clean (dry first, then wet-dry if needed), re-inspect and re-test. A UPC end face at -41.5 dB is almost certainly dirty or damaged, and it fails the carrier's 0.50 dB and -50 dB limits","Ignore it, because reflectance doesn't matter for CWDM"],
     answer:2, why:"Good UPC connectors reflect -50 to -55 dB, like the HUT-A end at -55.0 dB. Contamination is the number-one cause of high loss and reflectance, and the carrier's spec is tighter than TIA's. Never mate APC to UPC, because the 8-degree face against a flat face adds decibels of loss and can damage the ferrules."},
    {id:"fcp24q4", q:"After re-racking the HH-14 slack, which test proves the repair?",
     options:["The splicer's estimated loss for the section","Bidirectional OTDR at 1310 and 1550 nm (and 1625 nm if available) showing event 4 gone or under 0.15 dB averaged, plus OLTS at both wavelengths on every fiber in the cable, saved as .sor files with the as-built and a photo of the coil","A VFL check showing no red glow in HH-14","A 1310 nm OLTS test only, since the standard accepts one wavelength"],
     answer:1, why:"Only an OTDR locates and measures the event, and only a 1550 nm (or longer) test can see a bend that hides at 1310 nm. The OLTS proves the end-to-end budget. A VFL cannot show a few tenths of a dB through an OSP jacket, no splice was made so there is no splicer estimate, and a 1310-only test is exactly the test that missed this fault."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the trouble report for the engineer. Give the fault location and cause with the trace evidence, list every other finding on fiber 7 and what you will do about each, and describe the repair, the re-test that proves it, and what changes on the as-built.",
    rubric:["Locates the primary fault at HH-14: event 4 at about 1,847 m of fiber, which matches 6,000 ft of sheath once about 1% excess fiber length is allowed, and links it to the slack loop re-coiled into the undersized box.","Identifies a macrobend from the wavelength signature (0.42 dB at 1310 nm vs 2.99 dB at 1550 nm averaged, non-reflective, the same from both ends, affecting the whole tube) and notes that CWDM channels up to 1611 nm would lose even more.","Treats event 5 as a gainer, reports the bidirectional average (0.05 dB), and takes no action on it.","Flags the HUT-B connector (0.74 dB, -41.5 dB) against the carrier spec and plans inspect, clean, inspect on both sides, replacing parts only if cleaning fails.","Specifies the repair (a correctly sized box or slack bracket, the 30 ft coil re-racked at no less than 10x the 0.60 in OD, which is a 6 in radius, and no tight zip ties) and the re-test (bidirectional OTDR at 1310 and 1550 nm, plus OLTS at both wavelengths on all fibers against the 4.3 dB budget).","Updates the as-built with the box change, a photo of the coil and the new trace files."],
    reference:"Fiber 7 fails at 1550 nm because of a macrobend at HH-14. OTDR event 4 is non-reflective, sits 1,847 m of fiber from the HUT-A connector, and loses 0.42 dB at 1310 nm but 2.99 dB at 1550 nm (bidirectional averages). Loss that rises that steeply with wavelength at a single point is bend loss. HH-14 is at 6,000 ft of sheath (1,829 m), and with about 1% excess fiber length that is 1,847 m of fiber. The as-built note explains it: the 30 ft slack loop was re-coiled tighter and zip-tied to fit a 17 x 30 in. box, but a 0.60 in cable needs a 6 in radius at rest. The rest of tube 1 shows the same 1550 nm excess, and the 1611 nm CWDM channel would lose more. Event 5 at HH-19 is a gainer (-0.07 dB from A, +0.17 dB from B). Its true loss is the 0.05 dB average, so it needs no work. The HUT-B connector (0.74 dB, -41.5 dB) fails the carrier's 0.50 dB and -50 dB limits, so I will inspect, clean and re-inspect both sides. Repair: fit a correctly sized box or slack bracket and re-rack the coil at a 6 in radius or more, without tight ties. Re-test every fiber with bidirectional OTDR at 1310 and 1550 nm and OLTS at both wavelengths against the 4.3 dB budget. Expect about 1.4–1.9 dB at 1550 nm. Attach the .sor files and photos to the updated as-built.",
    alternatives:["Cutting out the bent section and splicing in new cable is acceptable if the cable is kinked or the jacket is damaged at HH-14. It adds two splices to the budget and the as-built, though, and isn't needed if re-racking clears the loss.","Blaming the 1550 nm failure on the HUT-B connector is only partly right. The connector is out of the carrier's spec and must be cleaned, but its loss is nearly flat with wavelength, so it cannot explain the 2.2 dB gap between the 1310 and 1550 nm results.","Asking the engineer to accept the link based on the 1310 nm pass misses the point: the CWDM plan runs from 1471 to 1611 nm, where bend loss is worst."] },
  hints:["Compare each event's loss at 1310 and 1550 nm, and compare each event as seen from both ends.","What kind of loss grows sharply with wavelength? Convert event 4's distance to sheath feet and find that point on the as-built.","Event 4 is a macrobend in the re-coiled HH-14 slack loop, and event 5 is a gainer (average 0.05 dB). Clean the HUT-B connector, re-rack the loop, and re-test from both ends at both wavelengths."] },

{ id:"fcp25", kind:"case", title:"Half the API clients fail after a certificate renewal", level:3, domains:["p25"],
  context:"You run the API tier for a field-service company. HAProxy at 10.20.0.10 passes TCP 443 straight through (round robin, no TLS termination) to two nginx nodes, web01 (10.20.0.11) and web02 (10.20.0.12), serving api.example.com. The certificate was due to expire on 29 October, so on Sunday evening a colleague installed the renewed certificate on both nodes from the CA's download bundle. Since Monday morning the technicians' Android app, a Python billing job and the curl-based monitors fail about half the time, while staff using Edge on Windows laptops see no problem. Customer integrations call the same endpoint and the support queue is filling. You need the cause, a fix that holds, and proof that it's fixed.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Client-side errors, Monday 08:00-09:30",
     text:"billing-job (Python 3.12, requests):\n  requests.exceptions.SSLError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='api.example.com', port=443):\n  Max retries exceeded with url: /v2/invoices (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1,\n  '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: unable to get local issuer\n  certificate (_ssl.c:1010)')))\nmonitor (curl on Ubuntu 24.04):\n  curl: (60) SSL certificate problem: unable to get local issuer certificate\nAndroid app (OkHttp):\n  javax.net.ssl.SSLHandshakeException: java.security.cert.CertPathValidatorException:\n  Trust anchor for certification path not found.\nFailure rate: billing 47% | monitor 52% | Android 49% | Edge on Windows 0%\nTicket comment: \"Edge works, so the cert is fine. Must be the Android app.\""},
    {label:"openssl s_client against each backend, from a Linux admin host (filtered)",
     text:"$ openssl s_client -connect 10.20.0.11:443 -servername api.example.com </dev/null\ndepth=0 CN = api.example.com\nverify error:num=20:unable to get local issuer certificate\nverify error:num=21:unable to verify the first certificate\n 0 s:CN = api.example.com\n   i:C = US, O = Northwind Trust, CN = Northwind TLS RSA CA 2025\n   v:NotBefore: Oct 25 00:00:00 2026 GMT; NotAfter: May 12 23:59:59 2027 GMT\nNo client certificate CA names sent\nVerify return code: 21 (unable to verify the first certificate)\n\n$ openssl s_client -connect 10.20.0.12:443 -servername api.example.com </dev/null\ndepth=2 C = US, O = Northwind Trust, CN = Northwind Root R1\ndepth=1 C = US, O = Northwind Trust, CN = Northwind TLS RSA CA 2025\ndepth=0 CN = api.example.com\n 0 s:CN = api.example.com\n   i:C = US, O = Northwind Trust, CN = Northwind TLS RSA CA 2025\n   v:NotBefore: Apr 12 00:00:00 2026 GMT; NotAfter: Oct 29 23:59:59 2026 GMT\n 1 s:C = US, O = Northwind Trust, CN = Northwind TLS RSA CA 2025\n   i:C = US, O = Northwind Trust, CN = Northwind Root R1\nNo client certificate CA names sent\nVerify return code: 0 (ok)"},
    {label:"On the servers",
     text:"web01$ grep ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/conf.d/api.conf\n    ssl_certificate      /etc/nginx/ssl/api.crt;\n    ssl_certificate_key  /etc/nginx/ssl/api.key;\nweb01$ grep -c 'BEGIN CERTIFICATE' /etc/nginx/ssl/api.crt\n1\nCA bundle contents: api_example_com.crt (leaf), Northwind-TLS-RSA-CA-2025.crt (intermediate)\n\nweb02$ sudo tail -1 /var/log/nginx/error.log\n2026/10/25 21:14:02 [emerg] 1188#1188: SSL_CTX_use_PrivateKey(\"/etc/nginx/ssl/api.key\") failed\n  (SSL: error:05800074:x509 certificate routines::key values mismatch)\nweb02$ openssl crl2pkcs7 -nocrl -certfile /etc/nginx/ssl/api.crt | openssl pkcs7 -print_certs -noout | grep subject\nsubject=C = US, O = Northwind Trust, CN = Northwind TLS RSA CA 2025\nsubject=CN = api.example.com\nweb02$ openssl x509 -in api_example_com.crt -noout -pubkey | sha256sum\n9c41e07b5a...d27a  -\nweb02$ openssl pkey -in /etc/nginx/ssl/api.key -pubout | sha256sum\n9c41e07b5a...d27a  -\nColleague's note: \"s_client says No client certificate CA names sent. Did the renewal break client certs?\""}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp25q1", q:"Against web01, why does Edge on Windows succeed while curl, Python and Android fail?",
     options:["Windows trusts Northwind Root R1, but the Linux and Android trust stores don't","web01 sends only the leaf. The Windows verifier fetches the missing intermediate from the leaf's Authority Information Access URL (or uses a cached copy), while OpenSSL, Python and Android build the path only from what the server sends plus their root stores","Edge negotiates TLS 1.3 and the other clients use TLS 1.2, which needs a different chain","The billing job's certifi bundle is out of date"],
     answer:1, why:"From the same Linux host, web02's chain verifies to Northwind Root R1 with return code 0, so the root is trusted there. web01's file has one certificate and s_client shows depth 0 only, with errors 20 and 21. Edge succeeding only proves that one client can repair a broken chain, not that the server is configured correctly."},
    {id:"fcp25q2", q:"On web02, nginx reports 'key values mismatch', yet the leaf's public key and the private key hash identically. What happened?",
     options:["The CA issued the renewal for a new key, and the old key was installed","The private key file is protected by a passphrase","The certificate is ECDSA and the key is RSA","The combined file lists the intermediate first. nginx checks the key against the first certificate in ssl_certificate, so the leaf must come first, followed by the intermediate"],
     answer:3, why:"The matching public-key hashes prove the key belongs to the leaf. The file's first certificate is the intermediate, though, and the key doesn't match that one. The fix is ordering, not a new key or CSR. A passphrase or algorithm problem would give a different error."},
    {id:"fcp25q3", q:"Why do only about half of requests fail, and why is that worse than it looks?",
     options:["HAProxy sends half the connections to web02, which still serves the old certificate with a correct chain because the failed reload left nginx running its previous configuration. That certificate expires on 29 October, and after that web02 fails every client too","TLS session resumption lets half the clients skip certificate verification","Half the clients already have the intermediate cached","HAProxy terminates TLS for half of the traffic"],
     answer:0, why:"When a reload fails, nginx keeps its old workers and configuration, so web02 still presents the April certificate (NotAfter 29 October) with its chain. Round robin splits clients roughly 50/50, which matches the 47–52% failure rates. In three days the 'working' node fails too. HAProxy is in TCP passthrough, and resumption doesn't skip chain validation on a full handshake."},
    {id:"fcp25q4", q:"Which change and verification fix this properly?",
     options:["Turn off certificate verification in the billing job and monitors until the CA fixes its bundle","Install Northwind's root certificate on every client","On both nodes, serve the leaf followed by the intermediate (not the root) in one file, run nginx -t, reload, then test each backend by IP with openssl s_client -verify_return_error and confirm Verify return code 0 and NotAfter May 2027","Add the intermediate to ssl_trusted_certificate in nginx"],
     answer:2, why:"The server must send the full chain in the right order. Testing by backend IP catches a node the VIP hides, and nginx -t catches ordering and key errors before a reload. ssl_trusted_certificate is used for OCSP stapling and client-certificate verification and doesn't add anything to the chain sent to clients. Turning off verification removes the protection TLS exists to provide."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the incident note. Give the root cause on each node with the evidence that proves it, explain why different clients behave differently, and describe the fix for both nodes, how you will verify it per backend, and what you'll change in the renewal procedure so this can't happen again.",
    rubric:["Shows that web01 serves only the leaf (one BEGIN CERTIFICATE, s_client depth 0, verify errors 20 and 21) and that the missing piece is the intermediate, not the root.","Explains the client differences: Edge on Windows completes the chain through AIA fetching or caching, while OpenSSL, Python requests and Android do not, so they report an unknown issuer.","Diagnoses web02: the intermediate-first file made nginx compare the key with the wrong certificate ('key values mismatch' even though the public-key hashes match), the reload failed, and the old certificate expiring 29 October is still being served.","Fixes both nodes with a leaf-then-intermediate file (root excluded), runs nginx -t before reloading, and verifies each backend IP with s_client -verify_return_error, expecting return code 0 and the new NotAfter.","Dismisses 'No client certificate CA names sent' (the server doesn't request client certificates) and the 'Edge works, so it's Android' theory, with evidence.","Prevents recurrence: automated deployment that installs the full chain, reloads gated on nginx -t, and chain and expiry monitoring on every backend, not only through the VIP."],
    reference:"Two different mistakes, hidden behind round robin. web01's new certificate file contains only the leaf (s_client: depth 0, errors 20 and 21), so clients that build the path only from what the server sends plus their root store (curl/OpenSSL, Python requests, Android) can't link the leaf to Northwind Root R1. Edge on Windows succeeds because the Windows verifier fetches the missing intermediate from the leaf's AIA URL or has it cached. The root is fine: web02 verifies with return code 0 from the same Linux host. On web02 the colleague put the intermediate before the leaf. nginx matches the private key against the first certificate in the file, so the reload failed with 'key values mismatch'. The key is correct; its public-key hash matches the leaf's. nginx kept its old configuration, so web02 still serves the April certificate with a full chain. That's why about half the requests work, and that certificate expires on 29 October. 'No client certificate CA names sent' only means the server doesn't ask for client certificates. Fix: on both nodes, build api.crt as the leaf followed by the intermediate (no root), run nginx -t, reload, and check the error log. Then test each backend by IP with openssl s_client -connect 10.20.0.11:443 -servername api.example.com -verify_return_error. Expect depth 0 and 1, return code 0 and NotAfter May 12 2027. Prevention: deploy renewals with automation that installs the full chain, gate reloads on nginx -t, and monitor chain validity and expiry on every backend.",
    alternatives:["Terminate TLS on HAProxy with one correct full-chain file. That removes per-node drift, but the same ordering check and per-backend verification still apply to any TLS on the internal hop.","Adding the intermediate to client trust stores (the Android app's network security config, the billing job's CA bundle) works around web01 for those clients only. Every customer integration still fails, and it hides the server misconfiguration.","Fixing web01 alone is only partly right. Failures stop today, but web02 is still on the old certificate and fails every client after 29 October."] },
  hints:["Run the same s_client command against each backend IP instead of the VIP, and compare the chains and dates.","Count the certificates each node sends. Which clients fetch a missing intermediate by themselves? On web02, which certificate does nginx compare the key with?","web01 is missing the intermediate. web02's file is in the wrong order, so its reload failed and the old certificate (expires 29 October) is still live. Serve leaf plus intermediate on both, run nginx -t, reload, and verify each IP."] },

{ id:"fcp26", kind:"case", title:"SLA attainment fell to 82% and the manager wants two hires", level:1, domains:["p26"],
  context:"You're the senior L2 technician on the service desk for a county government of about 900 staff. Business hours are 08:00–17:00 on weekdays. October's SLA attainment came in at 82.4%, down from 95.1% in September. The IT manager has drafted a request for two more L1 technicians and wants you to back it with data before Friday's budget meeting. A few things changed in early October. A new self-service portal form went live on 1 October, the ticketing platform was upgraded on 4 October, and on 6 October the sysadmins moved drive mappings from a logon script to Group Policy Preferences. Find out what is actually driving the breaches and recommend what to do.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"SLA report by priority, September vs October",
     text:"Priority  Sep due  Sep met  Sep %   Oct due  Oct met  Oct %   Resolution target\nP1        3        3        100.0   4        4        100.0   4 h, 24x7\nP2        96       90       93.8    402      251      62.4    8 business hours\nP3        790      754      95.4    868      762      87.8    3 business days\nP4        351      332      94.6    336      310      92.3    5 business days\nTotal     1,240    1,179    95.1    1,610    1,327    82.4\n\nOct P2 by channel: portal 306 (of which 'Who is affected? Just me' = 291) | phone 71 | email 25\nOct P2 breaches:   portal 118 | phone and email 33\nL1 headcount: 6 in September, 6 in October (one technician on leave 13-16 Oct)"},
    {label:"Portal form workflow (live 1 Oct) and the SLA policy's priority matrix",
     text:"Portal form 'Report a problem'\n  Field: 'How urgent is this for you?'   Low | Medium | High\n  Field: 'Who is affected?'              Just me | My team | Whole department or site\n  Workflow rule: priority = urgency (High -> P2, Medium -> P3, Low -> P4)\n  'Who is affected?' is stored on the ticket but not used by any rule\n\nSLA policy, priority matrix (impact x urgency)\n                       Urgency High   Urgency Medium   Urgency Low\nImpact High (dept/site) P1             P2               P3\nImpact Medium (team)    P2             P3               P4\nImpact Low (one user)   P3             P4               P4"},
    {label:"Category trend and sample tickets",
     text:"Category                       Sep   Oct\nMapped drive / share access    41    288\nPassword / lockout             175   190\nPrinting                       122   131\nSoftware request               164   158\n\nINC0041877 portal P2 'S: drive missing after restart, red X'\n   Resolved: gpupdate /force, sign out and back in (workaround)\nINC0041903 phone  P3 'Shared drive gone again this morning'\n   Resolved: same workaround\nINC0042210 portal P2 'S and H drives disconnected'\n   Resolved: same workaround. Sysadmin note: 'GPP drive maps need \"Always wait for\n   the network at computer startup and logon\". It isn't set, so maps often apply\n   only on the second logon.'\nMapped-drive tickets closed with this workaround in October: 281 of 288\nProblem records opened in October: 0"},
    {label:"Platform upgrade note and SLA timer audit",
     text:"Release note, ticketing platform 2026.10 (installed 4 Oct):\n  'SLA engine rewritten for performance. Existing SLA policies migrated automatically.'\nTicket comment from a colleague: 'The new SLA engine is miscounting. Roll it back.'\nAudit log: SLA policies or business-hours calendars changed since 1 Sep: none\nCalendar 'County Standard': Mon-Fri 08:00-17:00 (unchanged)\nSpot check: 20 breached October tickets, business-hours elapsed time recomputed\n  by hand from the ticket timestamps: matches the platform's timer in 20 of 20"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp26q1", q:"Why did P2 volume jump from 96 to 402?",
     options:["Department-wide outages really did increase in October","The upgraded SLA engine re-labeled existing tickets as P2","The portal form sets priority from the user's urgency answer alone. 306 portal tickets became P2, and 291 of them affect one person, which the policy's matrix rates P3","Users switched from email to the phone"],
     answer:2, why:"Phone and email P2s add up to 96, the same as September's total, so genuine P2 demand didn't change. The extra 306 all came through the portal, where High urgency maps straight to P2 and the impact answer is ignored. P1 volume barely moved, so there was no rise in department-wide outages. Priority is impact times urgency, not how urgent the requester feels."},
    {id:"fcp26q2", q:"288 mapped-drive tickets were closed with the same workaround. How should the desk treat them in ITIL terms?",
     options:["288 incidents linked to one problem record. Publish the workaround as a known error and KB article, and fix the cause (the Group Policy setting) through a change","288 separate problems, one per affected user","One major incident, with all tickets merged and closed together","Service requests, because users are asking for access to a share"],
     answer:0, why:"Each user's interruption is an incident and is restored by the workaround. The shared cause is one problem, and once it's diagnosed it's a known error with a published workaround. The permanent fix is a change to the GPO. Zero problem records in a month with 288 repeat incidents is the process gap. A service request is a normal ask for something new, not a broken service."},
    {id:"fcp26q3", q:"A colleague blames the 4 October upgrade ('SLA engine rewritten'). How do you treat that?",
     options:["Roll back the upgrade before Friday","As the most likely cause, because the drop started the same week","Ask the vendor to recalculate October's SLA figures","As a red herring: no SLA policy or calendar changed, and 20 of 20 hand-recomputed breach timers match the platform. The breaches are real, just not the platform's fault"],
     answer:3, why:"Timing alone doesn't make a cause. The audit and the hand recomputation test the hypothesis directly, and it fails. Rolling back would cost effort and risk without changing a single breach, because the breaches come from what was logged and how it was prioritized."},
    {id:"fcp26q4", q:"What do you recommend to the manager for Friday?",
     options:["Approve both L1 hires now, because volume rose 30%","Fix intake first (apply the impact x urgency matrix to portal tickets and re-triage open P2s), open a problem for the drive maps and fix the GPO through a change, then re-measure in November and size staffing from that data","Relax the P2 resolution target to 3 business days","Tell technicians to close tickets before users confirm the fix"],
     answer:1, why:"About 247 of October's 370 extra tickets come from one fixable problem, and the P2 collapse comes from a form rule. Hiring on those numbers pays permanently for a temporary defect. Relaxing the target or closing early only hides the signal and lowers service quality."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write a one-page analysis for the IT manager. Explain with the numbers what drove October's drop, which theories the data rules out, what to change (intake, problem and change records, knowledge), how you'll measure the effect, and your recommendation on the two hires.",
    rubric:["Shows that P2 grew from 96 to 402, that 306 came through the portal where priority equals the user's urgency answer, that 291 of those were single-user tickets the matrix rates P3, and that portal P2s caused 118 of the 151 P2 breaches.","Identifies the drive-map surge (41 to 288, nearly all closed with one workaround) as a single problem with no problem record, ties it to the 6 October GPO change, and proposes a problem record, a known-error KB article and a change to fix the Group Policy setting.","Explains why genuine P2s suffered too: the 96 real P2s were buried in an inflated queue, and 33 of them breached.","Rules out the platform upgrade with the evidence (no policy or calendar changes, 20 of 20 timers match) and keeps the four days of leave in proportion.","Recommends fixing intake and the root cause before hiring, with a measurable November target and a date to revisit staffing.","Writes for the manager: plain numbers, ITIL terms used correctly (incident, problem, known error, change), and no blame on individuals."],
    reference:"October's 82.4% isn't a staffing problem yet. It's an intake problem plus one unmanaged problem. P2 tickets rose from 96 to 402. Phone and email P2s held at 96, while 306 came through the new portal form, which sets priority from the user's urgency answer alone. 291 of those affect only the requester, which our own impact x urgency matrix rates P3. Portal P2s caused 118 of the 151 P2 breaches, and burying the 96 genuine P2s in that queue made 33 of them breach too. Second, mapped-drive tickets jumped from 41 to 288 after the 6 October move to Group Policy Preferences. Nearly all were closed with the same gpupdate-and-sign-in workaround, and no problem record was opened. The sysadmin note gives the likely cause: 'Always wait for the network at computer startup and logon' isn't set, so drive maps often apply only on the second logon. That's about 247 extra tickets, two-thirds of October's 370-ticket increase. The 4 October upgrade is a red herring: no SLA policy or calendar changed, and 20 of 20 hand-checked timers match. Actions: make the portal workflow apply the matrix and re-triage open P2s; open a problem record, publish a known-error KB article, and fix the GPO through a normal change; track SLA and drive-map volume weekly through November. Recommendation: defer the two hires and revisit staffing with November's data. If attainment stays under 92% once intake is fixed, the case for hiring is real.",
    alternatives:["Keep the urgency question but make the workflow compute priority from both urgency and impact, with L1 confirming at triage. This is equivalent, as long as the impact field is actually used.","Bringing in one temporary L1 for November while the fixes land is defensible if the backlog is large. Making permanent hires on October's inflated numbers is not.","Fixing only the portal form is partly right. P2 attainment recovers, but the drive-map problem keeps generating about 55 tickets a week until the GPO is fixed."] },
  hints:["Split October's P2s by channel and compare them with September. What's different about the portal tickets?","Look at how the portal sets priority, and at the category that grew sevenfold. How many problem records were opened?","Priority = user urgency inflated P2 (291 single-user tickets), and the GPO drive-map change produced about 247 repeat incidents with no problem record. Fix intake and the root cause before hiring."] },

{ id:"fcp27", kind:"case", title:"The new 10G link that made backups slower", level:3, domains:["p27"],
  context:"Your company's HQ and data center are joined by two routed links between HQ-R1 and DC-R2, both IOS XE routers in single-area OSPF. One is the old 1 Gbps circuit on Gi0/0/1. The other is a new 10 Gbps dark-fiber link on Te0/1/0, turned up on Saturday. The new link was bought for the nightly backup: a single TCP stream from the HQ file server (10.10.20.15) to the DC backup target (10.50.8.20) that used to take over six hours at about 900 Mb/s. Last night it took just as long, and HQ users complained that DC applications were sluggish all evening. Last week a colleague tuned OSPF on DC-R2. Your manager asks why the 10G link isn't being used and how to fix it without losing the 1G path as a backup.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"HQ-R1 (Lo0 rows omitted)",
     text:"HQ-R1# show ip ospf interface brief\nInterface    PID   Area   IP Address/Mask    Cost  State Nbrs F/C\nTe0/1/0      1     0      10.255.0.5/30      1     P2P   1/1\nGi0/0/1      1     0      10.255.0.1/30      1     P2P   1/1\nTe0/0/0      1     0      10.10.16.1/20      1     DR    2/2\nHQ-R1# show ip route 10.50.8.20\nRouting entry for 10.50.8.0/22\n  Known via \"ospf 1\", distance 110, metric 11, type intra area\n  Last update from 10.255.0.6 on TenGigabitEthernet0/1/0, 13:02:41 ago\n  Routing Descriptor Blocks:\n    10.255.0.6, from 10.255.255.2, 13:02:41 ago, via TenGigabitEthernet0/1/0\n      Route metric is 11, traffic share count is 1\n  * 10.255.0.2, from 10.255.255.2, 13:02:41 ago, via GigabitEthernet0/0/1\n      Route metric is 11, traffic share count is 1\nHQ-R1# show ip cef exact-route 10.10.20.15 10.50.8.20\n10.10.20.15 -> 10.50.8.20 =>IP adj out of GigabitEthernet0/0/1, addr 10.255.0.2"},
    {label:"DC-R2 (Lo0 rows omitted) and the colleague's change record",
     text:"DC-R2# show running-config | section router ospf\nrouter ospf 1\n router-id 10.255.255.2\n auto-cost reference-bandwidth 100000\n passive-interface default\n no passive-interface GigabitEthernet0/0/1\n no passive-interface TenGigabitEthernet0/1/0\nDC-R2# show ip ospf interface brief\nInterface    PID   Area   IP Address/Mask    Cost  State Nbrs F/C\nTe0/1/1      1     0      10.50.8.1/22       10    DR    0/0\nTe0/1/0      1     0      10.255.0.6/30      10    P2P   1/1\nGi0/0/1      1     0      10.255.0.2/30      100   P2P   1/1\nDC-R2# show ip route 10.10.20.15 | include metric|via\n  Known via \"ospf 1\", distance 110, metric 11, type intra area\n  * 10.255.0.5, from 10.255.255.1, 13:03:10 ago, via TenGigabitEthernet0/1/0\n      Route metric is 11, traffic share count is 1\nCHG-2219 (19 Oct), DC-R2: 'Set reference bandwidth so 10G costs correctly.\n  IOS printed: % OSPF: Reference bandwidth is changed. Please ensure reference\n  bandwidth is consistent across all routers. HQ-R1 to follow.' Status: closed"},
    {label:"HQ-R1 counters at 23:40 (during the backup) and the backup job",
     text:"HQ-R1# show interfaces Gi0/0/1 | include load|drops|rate\n  reliability 255/255, txload 249/255, rxload 1/255\n  Input queue: 0/375/0/0 (size/max/drops/flushes); Total output drops: 1284553\n  5 minute input rate 41000 bits/sec, 52 packets/sec\n  5 minute output rate 978204000 bits/sec, 81377 packets/sec\nHQ-R1# show interfaces Te0/1/0 | include load|drops|rate|unknown\n  reliability 255/255, txload 3/255, rxload 31/255\n  Input queue: 0/375/0/0 (size/max/drops/flushes); Total output drops: 0\n  5 minute input rate 1214733000 bits/sec, 104118 packets/sec\n  5 minute output rate 121904000 bits/sec, 22950 packets/sec\n     41227 unknown protocol drops\nBackup job nightly-fs01: 2.60 TB in 06:24:11, average 112.8 MB/s\nfs01 and bk01 NICs: 10000Mb/s, full duplex, no errors"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp27q1", q:"Why does HQ-R1 give both links an OSPF cost of 1?",
     options:["OSPF on IOS XE uses hop count, not bandwidth","Cost is the reference bandwidth divided by the interface bandwidth, with a minimum of 1, and the default reference is 100 Mbps, so 1 Gbps and 10 Gbps both cost 1. HQ-R1 never got the 100000 reference that DC-R2 has","Te0/1/0 is missing a 'bandwidth' command, so IOS assumes 1 Gbps","OSPF always costs point-to-point links at 1"],
     answer:1, why:"100 Mbps / 1,000 Mbps and 100 / 10,000 both round up to the minimum cost of 1. DC-R2, with a 100 Gbps reference, computes 100 for Gi and 10 for Te, exactly as the course formula predicts. Hop count is RIP's metric, and P2P network type only skips the DR election; it doesn't fix the cost."},
    {id:"fcp27q2", q:"HQ-R1 installs both paths, so why is the backup still stuck near 900 Mb/s while Te0/1/0 is mostly idle outbound?",
     options:["The file server's NIC is only 1 Gbps","OSPF equal-cost routing sends packets round robin, so only half the backup goes over the 10G link","A TCP window can't carry more than 1 Gbps","CEF load-shares per flow by source and destination. The backup flow hashes onto Gi0/0/1 (exact-route), so one stream can never exceed that 1 Gbps link, and every other flow that hashes there queues behind it, hence 1.28 million output drops"],
     answer:3, why:"Per-destination (per-flow) load sharing keeps each flow on one path so packets don't reorder. exact-route shows which path this flow got. Gi0/0/1 is at 249/255 with heavy drops, which is why other HQ-to-DC traffic felt slow too. The servers have 10GbE NICs, and nothing about TCP caps it at 1 Gbps."},
    {id:"fcp27q3", q:"Why does traffic from the DC to HQ use only Te0/1/0?",
     options:["DC-R2 uses a reference of 100000, so its Gi0/0/1 costs 100 and Te0/1/0 costs 10. It has one best path (metric 11 vs 101), and the inconsistent reference bandwidth makes routing asymmetric","DC-R2's OSPF adjacency on Gi0/0/1 is down","OSPF always routes symmetrically, so the forward path must be wrong too","DC-R2 prefers the interface with the higher IP address"],
     answer:0, why:"Each OSPF router runs SPF with its own outgoing costs, so two routers with different reference bandwidths choose different paths and asymmetry follows. The adjacency is up (Nbrs 1/1 on Gi0/0/1). IOS even warned about this when CHG-2219 was applied."},
    {id:"fcp27q4", q:"What is the right fix?",
     options:["Shut down Gi0/0/1","Add a static route on HQ-R1 to 10.50.8.0/22 via Te0/1/0","Configure auto-cost reference-bandwidth 100000 under router ospf 1 on HQ-R1 and confirm it on every other router in the domain, so Te costs 10 and Gi costs 100 everywhere and the 1G link stays as an automatic backup","Raise maximum-paths so more flows spread across both links"],
     answer:2, why:"A consistent reference bandwidth makes the 10G path win in both directions (metric 20 vs 110) and keeps the 1G path in the database for failover. Shutting Gi0/0/1 removes the backup. A static route (AD 1) overrides OSPF and loses dynamic failover beyond the local interface. More ECMP paths spread more flows but never let one flow exceed one link."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the change proposal. Explain with the outputs why the backup and the DC applications were slow and which signals you ruled out. Give the exact configuration change and where it must go, how you'll verify it (including failover), and how you'll stop the same mismatch from happening again.",
    rubric:["Explains the cost calculation: the default 100 Mbps reference gives 1G and 10G a cost of 1 on HQ-R1, so it installs two equal-cost routes (metric 11), while DC-R2's 100000 reference gives Te 10 and Gi 100.","Uses show ip cef exact-route to show the backup flow is hashed onto Gi0/0/1, and explains per-flow load sharing (one flow never exceeds one link) and the output drops that slowed other HQ-to-DC flows.","Identifies the asymmetric routing (DC to HQ only over Te) caused by the inconsistent reference bandwidth, and links it to the half-finished CHG-2219.","Fixes it with auto-cost reference-bandwidth 100000 on HQ-R1 and every other router in the area, keeps Gi0/0/1 as an automatic backup, and rejects shutting the link, static routes or more ECMP paths.","Verifies with show ip ospf interface brief (Te 10, Gi 100), a single route via Te at metric 20, exact-route via Te0/1/0, the next backup well above 1 Gb/s, and a controlled failover test in a change window.","Dismisses the unknown protocol drops and the server NICs as causes, and prevents recurrence with a standard OSPF template or a config-compliance check."],
    reference:"The 10G link is only half used because the two routers disagree about what 10G costs. OSPF cost is the reference bandwidth divided by the interface bandwidth, with a minimum of 1. HQ-R1 still has the default 100 Mbps reference, so Gi0/0/1 and Te0/1/0 both cost 1 and it installs two equal-cost routes to 10.50.8.0/22 at metric 11. CEF load-shares per flow, and exact-route puts the backup (10.10.20.15 to 10.50.8.20) on Gi0/0/1. One TCP stream can't use more than one link, so the backup ran at its old 900 Mb/s, and every flow hashed onto Gi queued behind it: 249/255 txload and 1.28 million output drops, which is why DC apps felt slow. DC-R2 got a 100000 reference in CHG-2219, so it sees Te at 10 and Gi at 100 and returns everything over Te. That asymmetry is half of a change that was never finished. Neither the servers' 10GbE NICs nor the unknown protocol drops (frames the router doesn't process, such as LLDP when it's disabled) is the cause. Fix: in a change window, set auto-cost reference-bandwidth 100000 under router ospf 1 on HQ-R1 and confirm the same value on every router in area 0. Verify Te0/1/0 at cost 10 and Gi0/0/1 at 100, one route via Te at metric 20, exact-route via Te0/1/0, and the next backup well above 1 Gb/s. Then shut Te0/1/0 briefly and confirm failover to Gi at metric 110. Prevent: add the reference bandwidth to the OSPF template and config-compliance checks.",
    alternatives:["Setting ip ospf cost by hand on both routers' interfaces (Te 10, Gi 100) works and is symmetric, but it has to be repeated for every future link. A consistent reference bandwidth fixes the whole domain at once.","Bundling the two links isn't possible, because links of different speeds can't form an EtherChannel. Adding ECMP paths only spreads more flows. Neither lets a single stream go beyond 1 Gbps.","Shutting Gi0/0/1 makes tonight's backup fast but removes the backup path, so a cut in the dark fiber would cut HQ off from the DC."] },
  hints:["Compare each link's OSPF cost on HQ-R1 and on DC-R2. Should they match?","How is OSPF cost calculated, and what's the default reference? With two equal paths, how does CEF decide which link one flow uses?","HQ-R1 still uses the 100 Mbps reference, so both links cost 1 and the backup flow hashed onto Gi0/0/1. Set auto-cost reference-bandwidth 100000 on HQ-R1 (and every router), then verify costs, the route and failover."] },

{ id:"fcp28", kind:"case", title:"A drift-cleanup playbook that deleted the printer VLANs", level:4, domains:["p28","p27"],
  context:"You own network automation for a five-building campus with 48 Catalyst access switches. Ansible runs from a CI pipeline with NetBox as the source of truth, and AWX runs a nightly 02:00 'baseline' job. Last Thursday a pull request merged that changed the VLAN role's resource-module state from merged to overridden, to 'remove drift'. Reviewers liked it, lint, unit tests and Batfish passed, and the check-mode diff on the canary group showed no changes. At 07:30 this morning the printers, badge controllers and HVAC controllers in buildings C and D were offline, while buildings A, B and E were fine. The facilities manager wants an ETA, and your director wants to know how a green pipeline did this.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"PR #212 and the pipeline it passed",
     text:"--- a/roles/access_vlans/tasks/main.yml\n+++ b/roles/access_vlans/tasks/main.yml\n@@ -3,6 +3,6 @@\n - name: Ensure standard VLANs\n   cisco.ios.ios_vlans:\n     config: \"{{ netbox_vlans }}\"\n-    state: merged\n+    state: overridden\n\n# netbox_vlans is rendered from NetBox VLAN group 'campus-standard'\n# .github/workflows/network-ci.yml (excerpt)\n  - run: yamllint . && ansible-lint playbooks/\n  - run: pytest tests/unit\n  - run: python tests/batfish_checks.py build   # initIssues, undefinedReferences\n  - run: ansible-playbook playbooks/baseline.yml --check --diff --limit canary\n# inventory: group canary = acc-a-01, acc-a-02 (building A)"},
    {label:"AWX job 4471, 02:00 (excerpt, -v)",
     text:"TASK [access_vlans : Ensure standard VLANs] ***********************************\n[WARNING]: ansible-pylibssh not installed, falling back to paramiko\nok: [acc-a-01]\nok: [acc-a-02]\nok: [acc-b-01]\nchanged: [acc-c-01] => {\"commands\": [\"no vlan 40\", \"no vlan 41\"]}\nchanged: [acc-c-02] => {\"commands\": [\"no vlan 40\", \"no vlan 41\"]}\nchanged: [acc-c-03] => {\"commands\": [\"no vlan 40\", \"no vlan 41\"]}\n...\nchanged: [acc-d-06] => {\"commands\": [\"no vlan 40\", \"no vlan 41\"]}\nok: [acc-e-01]\nTASK [access_vlans : Save when modified] **************************************\nchanged: [acc-c-01] ... (14 hosts)\nPLAY RECAP ********************************************************************\nacc-a-01 : ok=3 changed=0 unreachable=0 failed=0 skipped=0 rescued=0 ignored=0\nacc-c-03 : ok=3 changed=2 unreachable=0 failed=0 skipped=0 rescued=0 ignored=0\nSummary: 48 hosts, 14 changed (acc-c-01..08, acc-d-01..06), 0 failed"},
    {label:"acc-c-03 now, NetBox, and an old drift report",
     text:"acc-c-03# show vlan brief | include ^(1|10|20|30|40|41|99) \n1    default          active    Gi1/0/48\n10   USERS            active    Gi1/0/1, Gi1/0/2, Gi1/0/3, Gi1/0/4\n20   VOICE            active\n30   PRINTERS         active\n99   MGMT             active\nacc-c-03# show interfaces status | include inactive\nGi1/0/31  HP-M507-C2-1     inactive     40           auto   auto 10/100/1000BaseTX\nGi1/0/32  HP-M507-C2-2     inactive     40           auto   auto 10/100/1000BaseTX\nGi1/0/40  BADGE-CTRL-C2    inactive     41           auto   auto 10/100/1000BaseTX\nGi1/0/41  AHU-3-BACNET     inactive     41           auto   auto 10/100/1000BaseTX\nacc-c-03# show interfaces Gi1/0/31 switchport | include Access Mode\nAccess Mode VLAN: 40 (Inactive)\n\nNetBox, VLAN group campus-standard: 1, 10, 20, 30, 99\nNetBox, sites bldg-c and bldg-d: no site-scoped VLANs defined\nDrift report, 2026-09-14 (no ticket opened):\n  'acc-c-*, acc-d-*: VLAN 40 PRINTERS-CD and 41 BMS on device, not in NetBox'"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp28q1", q:"Why did buildings C and D lose their printers and building systems?",
     options:["state: overridden makes each switch's VLAN table exactly match the list and deletes every VLAN not in it. NetBox's campus-standard group lacks VLANs 40 and 41, which exist only in C and D, so the job removed them and every access port in those VLANs went inactive","paramiko dropped the SSH session mid-change and left the switches half-configured","state: merged had already deleted the VLANs earlier, and overridden only exposed it","NetBox rate-limited the inventory plugin, so the VLAN list came back empty"],
     answer:0, why:"The job's own output shows 'no vlan 40' and 'no vlan 41' on exactly the 14 C and D switches. A port whose access VLAN no longer exists goes inactive, which is what show interfaces status shows. Every host reported ok or changed with zero failures, so no session dropped. An empty list would have tried to remove VLANs 10–99 everywhere, not just 40 and 41 in two buildings. merged never removes VLANs."},
    {id:"fcp28q2", q:"The PR's check-mode diff showed no changes. Why didn't it catch this?",
     options:["Check mode doesn't work with resource modules","It ran with --limit canary, against two building-A switches that have no VLANs outside the standard list. A diff only shows the hosts it runs against, and no C or D switch was in the sample","Batfish should have caught it and has a bug","ansible-lint flagged overridden as risky, but the warning was ignored"],
     answer:1, why:"Check mode with --diff would have shown 'no vlan 40/41' if it had touched any C or D switch. The sample wasn't representative of the variation between sites. Batfish's parse and undefined-reference questions can't know that devices outside the rendered snapshot depend on VLANs 40 and 41. This is a test-design gap, not a tool bug."},
    {id:"fcp28q3", q:"What is the fastest safe way to restore service?",
     options:["Rebuild NetBox from scratch before touching any switch","Log in to all 14 switches and type the VLANs back by hand","Disable the 02:00 schedule, revert PR #212, add VLANs 40 and 41 to NetBox as site-scoped VLANs for bldg-c and bldg-d, and run the role in merged state limited to the C and D switches. The ports come back as soon as their VLAN exists. Then verify the ports are active and the devices respond","Wait for tonight's 02:00 run to reconcile the drift"],
     answer:2, why:"Ports recover as soon as the VLAN is recreated. Doing it through the pipeline keeps it reviewed and logged, and fixing NetBox first stops the next run from deleting the VLANs again. Typing them by hand is fast but untracked, and the 02:00 job would remove them again. Tonight's run with overridden still in place would repeat the outage."},
    {id:"fcp28q4", q:"Which change keeps drift removal as a goal but would have stopped this?",
     options:["Ban the overridden state permanently","Add more Batfish questions to the pipeline","Make AWX prompt an operator before each host","Reconcile the drift report into NetBox before enforcing, run check-mode diffs against every host (or at least one per site), fail the job on destructive commands such as 'no vlan' unless the PR has an explicit approval, and add pyATS pre/post checks that no access port goes inactive"],
     answer:3, why:"Enforcing a source of truth is only as safe as its completeness, so reconcile first. A representative diff plus a destructive-change gate makes the blast radius visible and deliberate, and state checks catch what config analysis can't. Banning overridden gives up the goal, and per-host prompts don't scale and get clicked through."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the post-incident report. Trace the cause from the PR to the inactive ports, explain why every green check missed it, describe how you restored service, and specify the pipeline and source-of-truth changes that keep drift removal without this blast radius.",
    rubric:["Explains the cause chain: overridden removes any VLAN not in the list, NetBox lacked the C and D VLANs 40 and 41, the job ran 'no vlan 40/41' on 14 switches, and ports in a deleted VLAN go inactive.","Explains why the checks missed it: lint and unit tests check syntax and templates, Batfish ran only parse and undefined-reference questions, and the check-mode diff was limited to two building-A canaries with no extra VLANs.","Restores service safely and traceably: pauses the 02:00 schedule, reverts the PR, adds VLANs 40 and 41 to NetBox (site-scoped), re-runs limited to C and D, and verifies active ports and reachable devices.","Ties the root cause to source-of-truth quality: the September drift report was never reconciled, so enforcing NetBox enforced its gaps.","Proposes guardrails: diffs on every host or per site, a gate that fails on destructive commands without explicit approval, pyATS/Genie pre/post state checks, and canaries that cover each site's variation.","Dismisses the pylibssh warning as unrelated (a connection-library fallback; no host failed or went unreachable)."],
    reference:"PR #212 changed the VLAN role from merged to overridden. For ios_vlans that means 'make the switch's VLAN table exactly this list', and the list is NetBox's campus-standard group (1, 10, 20, 30, 99). Buildings C and D use two VLANs that a contractor created by hand and nobody entered in NetBox: 40 (PRINTERS-CD) and 41 (BMS). The September drift report listed them and no ticket was opened. At 02:00 AWX sent 'no vlan 40' and 'no vlan 41' to the 14 C and D switches. Every access port in those VLANs went inactive, which took out the printers, badge controllers and HVAC. Every check was green because none of them looked at the right thing. Lint and unit tests check syntax and templates, and Batfish ran only parse and undefined-reference questions. The check-mode diff did touch real devices, but only two building-A canaries with no extra VLANs. The pylibssh warning is a connection-library fallback; no host failed. Restore: disable the 02:00 schedule, revert PR #212, add VLANs 40 and 41 to NetBox as site-scoped VLANs for bldg-c and bldg-d, run the role in merged state limited to those switches, and verify no port is inactive and the devices respond. Prevention: reconcile drift into NetBox before enforcing anything. Run check-mode diffs on every host, or at least one per site, and fail on 'no vlan' lines without explicit approval. Add pyATS pre/post checks that no access port goes inactive. Then reintroduce overridden one site at a time.",
    alternatives:["Keep merged permanently and handle drift with a separate report-only job (state: gathered compared with NetBox) that opens tickets. This is safer, but extra VLANs stay forever unless someone acts on the tickets.","Using replaced instead of overridden only changes the VLANs in the list, so it wouldn't have deleted 40 and 41, but it also wouldn't remove drift, which was the PR's goal.","Re-adding the VLANs by hand on 14 switches is fast, but if NetBox isn't fixed and the schedule isn't paused, the next 02:00 run deletes them again."] },
  hints:["Read the job's commands for a building C switch, then compare them with what NetBox says that switch should have.","What does overridden do to VLANs that aren't in the list? Which hosts did the PR's check-mode diff actually run against?","overridden enforced an incomplete source of truth: VLANs 40 and 41 were never in NetBox, and the diff only covered two building-A canaries. Pause the schedule, revert, add the site VLANs, re-run merged on C and D, then add per-site diffs and a destructive-change gate."] },

{ id:"fcp29", kind:"case", title:"Server stuck in emergency mode after a disk was added", level:2, domains:["p29","p3"],
  context:"app01 is a RHEL 9 VM on the company's VMware cluster. It runs the warehouse inventory application, whose database and uploads live on a separate 60 GB data disk mounted at /data. On Friday the app team added an fstab line for a new local backup disk in advance, planning to partition it on Monday. On Saturday afternoon the virtualization team hot-added that 200 GB disk. The monthly patch job rebooted app01 at 02:00 on Sunday. It's now Monday 07:40: the warehouse can't reach the application, and the VM console says 'You are in emergency mode'. You have the root password and the console. The 01:30 and 01:45 cron jobs are the backup and its retention cleanup.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"journalctl -b (the failed boot, excerpt)",
     text:"Oct 25 02:03:09 app01 kernel: piix4_smbus 0000:00:07.3: SMBus Host Controller not enabled!\nOct 25 02:03:10 app01 kernel: sd 0:0:1:0: [sdb] 419430400 512-byte logical blocks: (215 GB/200 GiB)\nOct 25 02:03:10 app01 kernel: sd 0:0:2:0: [sdc] 125829120 512-byte logical blocks: (64.4 GB/60.0 GiB)\nOct 25 02:03:10 app01 kernel:  sdc: sdc1\nOct 25 02:03:11 app01 systemd[1]: Expecting device dev-sdb1.device - /dev/sdb1...\nOct 25 02:03:11 app01 systemd[1]: Expecting device dev-sdc1.device - /dev/sdc1...\nOct 25 02:03:12 app01 systemd[1]: Found device dev-sdc1.device - Virtual_disk data.\nOct 25 02:03:12 app01 systemd[1]: Mounting backup.mount - /backup...\nOct 25 02:03:12 app01 kernel: XFS (sdc1): Mounting V5 Filesystem 7d2e41c6-5b0a-4f7e-9c11-2a8e5d3f9b40\nOct 25 02:03:12 app01 kernel: XFS (sdc1): Ending clean mount\nOct 25 02:03:12 app01 systemd[1]: Mounted backup.mount - /backup.\nOct 25 02:04:41 app01 systemd[1]: dev-sdb1.device: Job dev-sdb1.device/start timed out.\nOct 25 02:04:41 app01 systemd[1]: Timed out waiting for device dev-sdb1.device - /dev/sdb1.\nOct 25 02:04:41 app01 systemd[1]: Dependency failed for data.mount - /data.\nOct 25 02:04:41 app01 systemd[1]: Dependency failed for local-fs.target - Local File Systems.\nOct 25 02:04:41 app01 systemd[1]: local-fs.target: Job local-fs.target/start failed with result 'dependency'.\nOct 25 02:04:41 app01 systemd[1]: Started emergency.service - Emergency Shell."},
    {label:"From the emergency shell",
     text:"# lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,LABEL,UUID,MOUNTPOINTS\nNAME           SIZE FSTYPE      LABEL UUID                                   MOUNTPOINTS\nsda             40G\n├─sda1         600M vfat              4C1A-9E07                              /boot/efi\n├─sda2           1G xfs               0b6f3d1e-2c4a-4e8b-a1f7-9d55c0e6b218   /boot\n└─sda3        38.4G LVM2_member       Zp3kQe-7Hx2-...\n  ├─rhel-root 34.4G xfs               5e0c9a77-...                           /\n  └─rhel-swap    4G swap              a3d1f2b9-...                           [SWAP]\nsdb            200G\nsdc             60G\n└─sdc1          60G xfs         data  7d2e41c6-5b0a-4f7e-9c11-2a8e5d3f9b40   /backup\n# df -h /backup\nFilesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\n/dev/sdc1        60G   47G   13G  79% /backup\n# ls /backup\npgdata  uploads"},
    {label:"/etc/fstab, cron and the change ticket",
     text:"# cat /etc/fstab\n/dev/mapper/rhel-root  /          xfs   defaults                    0 0\nUUID=0b6f3d1e-2c4a-4e8b-a1f7-9d55c0e6b218  /boot  xfs  defaults     0 0\nUUID=4C1A-9E07         /boot/efi  vfat  umask=0077,shortname=winnt  0 2\n/dev/mapper/rhel-swap  none       swap  defaults                    0 0\n/dev/sdb1              /data      xfs   defaults                    0 0\n# CHG0004127 new backup disk - fstab prepared Fri 10/23, disk attach Sat by virt team\n/dev/sdc1              /backup    xfs   defaults                    0 0\n\n# cat /etc/cron.d/app-backup\n30 1 * * * root /usr/local/bin/db_dump.sh /backup/nightly\n45 1 * * * root find /backup -type f -mtime +14 -delete\n\nCHG0004127 worklog: 'Sat 10/24 16:20 virt team: 200 GB disk hot-added to app01\n  on SCSI(0:1). No reboot needed.'"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp29q1", q:"Why did app01 stop in emergency mode?",
     options:["The XFS filesystem on the data disk is corrupt","The new 200 GB disk took the name sdb, so the data disk became sdc. fstab still mounts /dev/sdb1, which no longer exists. After 90 seconds systemd gave up, data.mount failed, local-fs.target failed with it, and the emergency shell started","The piix4_smbus error stopped the boot","SELinux blocked the mount of /data"],
     answer:1, why:"Device names follow probe order. The new disk sits at SCSI 0:1, ahead of the data disk at 0:2, so the names shifted. The journal shows the 90 s wait (02:03:11 to 02:04:41) and the dependency chain. The data filesystem mounted cleanly ('Ending clean mount'), just at the wrong mount point, so it isn't corrupt."},
    {id:"fcp29q2", q:"What is mounted on /backup right now, and why does it matter?",
     options:["The new backup disk, which is fine","Nothing; it's an empty directory on the root filesystem","A copy of /data made by the virtualization team","The production data filesystem (label data, UUID 7d2e…, 47 GB of pgdata and uploads), because Friday's speculative /dev/sdc1 line now matches the old data disk. If the server boots like this, the 01:30 dump writes into production data and the 01:45 find deletes every file in it older than 14 days"],
     answer:3, why:"lsblk shows sdc1 with label data, 60 GB at 79% used, holding pgdata and uploads, mounted on /backup. The new disk (sdb) has no partition or filesystem yet. The retention job was written for a dedicated backup disk, and pointed at the production filesystem it becomes a deletion job."},
    {id:"fcp29q3", q:"Which fstab change is correct?",
     options:["Change /dev/sdb1 to /dev/sdc1 for /data","Add nofail to the /data line so the server always boots","Mount /data by UUID=7d2e41c6-5b0a-4f7e-9c11-2a8e5d3f9b40 and comment out the /backup line until the new disk is partitioned and formatted (then add it by UUID with nofail). Run systemctl daemon-reload and mount -a, and check with findmnt --verify before rebooting","Delete the /backup line and reboot; the device names return to normal after a clean boot"],
     answer:2, why:"A UUID is stored in the filesystem and doesn't move when disks are added. /dev/sdc1 works only until the next hardware change. nofail on /data would let the server boot without it, and the app would write into an empty /data directory on the root filesystem. The names won't 'return to normal', because probe order is now fixed by the new disk's slot."},
    {id:"fcp29q4", q:"How should you treat 'piix4_smbus 0000:00:07.3: SMBus Host Controller not enabled!'?",
     options:["As a harmless message seen on VMware guests, where the emulated PIIX4 SMBus controller isn't enabled. It appears on every boot and has nothing to do with the mounts","As a failed virtual SCSI controller that caused the disk rename","As the first thing to fix: blacklist the module and rebuild the initramfs","As the reason /dev/sdb1 wasn't found"],
     answer:0, why:"The message is about an SMBus controller the VM doesn't use, and it appears in normal boots too. The SCSI disks were detected fine (sdb and sdc are both in the kernel log). Chasing it wastes the outage window."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the recovery and follow-up. Give the step-by-step recovery from the emergency shell, the root cause with evidence, what you protect before the server goes back into service, how you prove the fix survives a reboot, and how you'll set up the new disk and prevent a repeat.",
    rubric:["Explains the cause with evidence: the new disk at SCSI 0:1 became sdb, the data disk became sdc, /dev/sdb1 timed out after 90 seconds, and the data.mount failure took down local-fs.target.","Spots that the data filesystem (label data, UUID 7d2e…) is mounted on /backup through the speculative /dev/sdc1 line, and that the 01:30 dump and 01:45 retention find would write into and delete from production data.","Recovers safely: unmounts /backup, changes /data to its UUID, comments out the /backup line, runs systemctl daemon-reload and mount -a, checks findmnt /data and the application's files, then continues with systemctl default or a reboot.","Rejects unsafe shortcuts: /dev/sdc1 for /data (breaks again), nofail on /data (the app would write to the root filesystem), and booting with the /backup line still in place.","Proves persistence with findmnt --verify and a planned reboot, then confirms the application and the next backup run.","Sets up the new disk properly (GPT, one partition, mkfs.xfs -L backup, fstab by UUID with nofail, mount -a) and sets a rule: UUIDs only, fstab lines only after the device exists. Dismisses the piix4_smbus message."],
    reference:"Device names follow probe order, and the order changed. Saturday's 200 GB disk was hot-added at SCSI 0:1, so at Sunday's reboot it became sdb and the 60 GB data disk at 0:2 became sdc. fstab still says /dev/sdb1 for /data. That partition doesn't exist, so systemd waited 90 seconds (02:03:11 to 02:04:41), data.mount failed, local-fs.target failed with it, and the emergency shell started. Worse, Friday's speculative line '/dev/sdc1 /backup' now matches the old data disk: lsblk shows the label-data filesystem, 47 GB of pgdata and uploads, mounted on /backup. If the server went back into service like this, the 01:30 dump would write into production and the 01:45 'find /backup -mtime +14 -delete' would delete production files. The piix4_smbus line is a routine VMware message. Recovery from the emergency shell: umount /backup; change the /data line to UUID=7d2e41c6-5b0a-4f7e-9c11-2a8e5d3f9b40 /data xfs defaults 0 0; comment out the /backup line; systemctl daemon-reload; mount -a; check findmnt /data and ls /data/pgdata; then systemctl default and test the app. Don't fix it with /dev/sdc1, which breaks at the next change, or with nofail on /data, which lets the app write into an empty directory on /. Prove it with findmnt --verify and a planned reboot. Then set up sdb: GPT label, one partition, mkfs.xfs -L backup, an fstab line by UUID with nofail, and mount -a. Team rule: UUIDs only, and fstab lines only after the device exists.",
    alternatives:["Mounting /data by LABEL=data is acceptable and readable, as long as no other filesystem carries the same label. Cloned and restored disks often do.","Booting with systemd.unit=rescue.target from GRUB instead of using the emergency shell is fine, but you still have to unmount /backup and fix fstab before any normal boot.","Adding x-systemd.device-timeout=10s only makes the same failure happen faster. It doesn't fix the device name, and the /backup problem remains."] },
  hints:["Read the journal from 'Expecting device' to 'Started emergency.service'. Which device never appeared, and which one did?","Compare lsblk's names, sizes and labels with fstab. Which filesystem is mounted on /backup, and what will cron do to it tonight?","The new disk took sdb, so /dev/sdb1 is gone, and the data disk (sdc1) is mounted on /backup. Unmount it, mount /data by UUID, comment out /backup, daemon-reload, mount -a, then reboot to prove it."] },

{ id:"fcp30", kind:"case", title:"Every VM on the lab host paused at once", level:3, domains:["p30"],
  context:"You run a Proxmox VE host, pve01, for your small IT business. A 2 x 2 TB NVMe ZFS mirror called tank holds all the VM disks as zvols. The host runs a database VM (101), the customer portal (102), a DNS and DHCP VM (103), and a Windows file server (104) that also stores your clients' photo archives. At 03:12 the portal and database went down together. Proxmox shows 101, 102 and 104 in an io-error state, while 103 is still up. On Friday evening you started a library re-index on the file server that rewrites thumbnails and metadata for about 700 GB of photos. Daily ZFS snapshots run with 30-day retention, and nightly backups go to a separate Proxmox Backup Server (PBS). It's 07:15 and clients are calling.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"VM state and host log at 07:15",
     text:"root@pve01:~# for v in 101 102 103 104; do echo -n \"$v \"; qm status $v --verbose | grep qmpstatus; done\n101 qmpstatus: io-error\n102 qmpstatus: io-error\n103 qmpstatus: running\n104 qmpstatus: io-error\nroot@pve01:~# journalctl --since 03:00 | grep -i 'no space' | head -3\nOct 26 03:12:07 pve01 QEMU[1712]: kvm: block I/O error in device 'drive-scsi0': No space left on device (28)\nOct 26 03:12:41 pve01 QEMU[1931]: kvm: block I/O error in device 'drive-scsi1': No space left on device (28)\nOct 26 03:13:02 pve01 QEMU[1790]: kvm: block I/O error in device 'drive-scsi0': No space left on device (28)\nroot@pve01:~# zpool status -x\nall pools are healthy"},
    {label:"ZFS space and storage configuration",
     text:"root@pve01:~# zpool list tank\nNAME   SIZE  ALLOC   FREE  CKPOINT  EXPANDSZ   FRAG    CAP  DEDUP    HEALTH  ALTROOT\ntank  1.81T  1.76T  51.2G        -         -    58%    97%  1.00x    ONLINE  -\nroot@pve01:~# zfs list -r -o name,volsize,used,avail,usedbysnapshots,usedbydataset,usedbyrefreservation tank\nNAME                 VOLSIZE   USED  AVAIL  USEDSNAP  USEDDS  USEDREFRESERV\ntank                       -  1.76T      0         0     96K              0\ntank/vm-101-disk-0      250G   212G      0     38.1G    174G              0\ntank/vm-102-disk-0       80G  61.4G      0     9.20G   52.2G              0\ntank/vm-103-disk-0      100G   107G  86.6G     4.10G   16.4G          86.6G\ntank/vm-104-disk-0      100G  48.3G      0     6.20G   42.1G              0\ntank/vm-104-disk-1     2.40T  1.33T      0      672G    694G              0\ntank/vm-9000-disk-0      32G  7.20G      0         0   7.20G              0\nroot@pve01:~# grep -A4 'zfspool: tank' /etc/pve/storage.cfg\nzfspool: tank\n        pool tank\n        content images,rootdir\n        sparse 1\nYour notes: 'sparse 1' (thin provisioning) enabled 2026-04-02 to fit files01's 2.4 TB disk. VM 103 created 2026-01."},
    {label:"Snapshots of the file server's data disk, and other signals",
     text:"root@pve01:~# zfs list -t snapshot -o name,used,refer -s creation tank/vm-104-disk-1\nNAME                                         USED  REFER\ntank/vm-104-disk-1@autodaily-2026-09-27     19.8G   690G\ntank/vm-104-disk-1@autodaily-2026-09-28      3.1G   691G\n  ... 24 more daily snapshots, USED 1.2G-4.4G each ...\ntank/vm-104-disk-1@autodaily-2026-10-23      4.0G   701G\ntank/vm-104-disk-1@autodaily-2026-10-24     88.6G   702G\ntank/vm-104-disk-1@autodaily-2026-10-25    101.2G   699G\ntank/vm-104-disk-1@autodaily-2026-10-26      0.9G   694G\nfiles01 event log: 'Library re-index started Fri 10/23 18:02. 61% complete, about 430 GB rewritten.'\nPBS: last verified backups of 101, 102, 103 and 104: 2026-10-25 23:00\nWeb GUI banner: 'You do not have a valid subscription for this server.'\napt update: 'E: Failed to fetch https://enterprise.proxmox.com/debian/pve/dists/trixie/InRelease  401  Unauthorized'"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp30q1", q:"Why are 101, 102 and 104 paused while 103 keeps running?",
     options:["An NVMe drive is failing and only some VMs touch the bad blocks","The pool is effectively full. When a write to a thin zvol returns ENOSPC, QEMU pauses the VM instead of passing the error to the guest. 103's zvol was created before thin provisioning was enabled, so it still has a refreservation with 86.6G unused, and its writes have space guaranteed","Memory ballooning starved the three larger VMs","ZFS detected corruption and suspended I/O to those zvols"],
     answer:1, why:"The host log shows 'No space left on device (28)' from each paused VM's QEMU process, and zpool status reports healthy pools, which rules out disk failure and corruption. USEDREFRESERV and AVAIL 86.6G on vm-103 show its reservation. The thin zvols have no reservation, so they hit the wall together."},
    {id:"fcp30q2", q:"zpool list shows 51.2G free at 97% capacity, yet the thin zvols show AVAIL 0. Why?",
     options:["zpool list counts mirror redundancy as free space","The free figure is a cached value that updates hourly","ZFS keeps slop space, about 1/32 of the pool (roughly 58G here), for its own operations. Ordinary writes see the pool as full before the last few percent is used","The 51.2G is reserved for the deduplication table"],
     answer:2, why:"Slop space lets ZFS keep working (deleting snapshots, metadata updates) when users have filled it. With 51.2G free and about 58G of slop, nothing is left for normal writes. A mirror's ALLOC and FREE already reflect usable space, and dedup is off (1.00x)."},
    {id:"fcp30q3", q:"vm-104-disk-1 shows 672G of USEDSNAP, but the individual snapshots' USED values add up to far less. What follows?",
     options:["Each snapshot's USED counts only blocks unique to it. Blocks the re-index rewrote are still held by every snapshot older than the rewrite, so you only get them back by destroying that whole range. Dry-run with zfs destroy -nv on a snapshot range to see the reclaim first","The USEDSNAP column is wrong and should be ignored","Destroying the newest snapshot alone frees the 672G","Snapshots don't use pool space until a rollback"],
     answer:0, why:"Shared blocks belong to no single snapshot, so per-snapshot USED undercounts them, and deleting one snapshot frees little. The 430 GB of rewrites since Friday, plus a month of churn, is the 672G. A dry run shows exactly what a range deletion will reclaim before you commit."},
    {id:"fcp30q4", q:"What is the safest way to get the VMs running again now?",
     options:["Add a spare USB disk to the pool with zpool add tank /dev/sdX to get space immediately","Reboot the host to clear the io-error state","Run zpool scrub tank and wait for it to finish","Confirm last night's verified PBS backups, dry-run and then destroy the older range of vm-104-disk-1 snapshots, stop the re-index (or that disk's snapshots) from refilling the pool, then qm resume 101, 102 and 104 and check the database and guest filesystems"],
     answer:3, why:"Freeing real space is the only fix, and the verified backups mean the old snapshots aren't your only recovery point (a snapshot isn't a backup anyway). zpool add of a single disk creates a permanent, unprotected vdev, and losing it loses the whole pool. A reboot leaves the pool full, and a scrub checks integrity and frees nothing."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the incident note. Explain why the VMs paused (and why 103 didn't), where the space went with the numbers, the recovery steps in order with the checks between them, and the changes that stop a full pool from taking everything down again.",
    rubric:["Explains the pause: writes to thin zvols returned ENOSPC once the pool was effectively full, QEMU paused those VMs (io-error), and 103 kept running because its pre-April zvol is thick (86.6G of unused refreservation).","Reads the pool numbers correctly: 97% capacity with 51.2G free but AVAIL 0 because of slop space, and about 2.95T of volume sizes promised on about 1.75T of usable space.","Attributes the growth to vm-104-disk-1's snapshots (672G USEDSNAP) holding blocks the re-index rewrote, and explains why the per-snapshot USED values add up to far less (shared blocks).","Recovers in a safe order: confirm verified PBS backups, run zfs destroy -nv and then destroy enough of the older snapshot range, stop the re-index or that disk's snapshots from refilling the pool, qm resume, then check guest filesystems and the database.","Rejects dangerous shortcuts (zpool add of a single disk, deleting VM disks, rebooting the host) and dismisses the subscription banner and the enterprise-repository 401 as unrelated.","Prevents a repeat: a capacity alert at 80%, a limit on thin overcommit or reservations for critical VMs, snapshot retention sized to the churn, and bulk archives moved off the VM pool."],
    reference:"The pool ran out of space, and when a write returns ENOSPC, QEMU pauses the VM rather than fail the guest's write. That's the io-error state and the 'No space left on device (28)' lines from 03:12. 103 kept running because its zvol was created in January, before thin provisioning (sparse 1) was enabled in April. It still has a refreservation with 86.6G unused, so its own writes have guaranteed space. 97% and 51.2G free is no contradiction: ZFS holds back slop space (about 1/32 of the pool, roughly 58G), and datasets see AVAIL 0 before that is touched. The design risk is about 2.95T of volume sizes promised on about 1.75T. The trigger was files01's re-index. It has rewritten about 430 GB since Friday, and 30 daily snapshots of vm-104-disk-1 still hold the old blocks: 672G of USEDSNAP. Most of that is shared between snapshots, which is why the individual USED values add up to far less and why deleting one snapshot frees little. Recovery: confirm last night's verified PBS backups, dry-run zfs destroy -nv on the 09-27 to 10-24 range, then destroy it. Hold the re-index or that disk's snapshots until there is headroom, then qm resume 101, 102 and 104 and check the database and guest filesystems. Never zpool add a single disk: that vdev is permanent and unprotected. The subscription banner and the 401 are unrelated. Prevention: alert at 80%, limit overcommit, size retention to churn, and move archives to their own pool.",
    alternatives:["Temporarily removing 103's refreservation (zfs set refreservation=none) frees about 87G at once and is acceptable as a stopgap if 103 can tolerate it, but it makes 103 vulnerable too and doesn't address the snapshot growth.","Moving files01's data disk to a separate HDD pool or a NAS is the right long-term design, but not the fix at 07:15. Moving 1.3T takes hours, and the pool needs free space first.","Rolling vm-104-disk-1 back to a pre-re-index snapshot would free space, but it discards everything files01 wrote since Friday, including client uploads, and needs the clients' agreement."] },
  hints:["Compare zpool list with the AVAIL column in zfs list, and compare the total of the VOLSIZE column with the pool's size.","Which dataset's USEDSNAP is huge, and what has been rewriting that disk since Friday? Why is 103 different? Look at USEDREFRESERV and your storage note.","Snapshots of vm-104-disk-1 hold 672G of rewritten blocks on a thin pool at 97%. Confirm the PBS backups, dry-run and destroy the older snapshot range, resume the VMs, then add an 80% alert and limit thin overcommit."] },

{ id:"fcp31", kind:"case", title:"Logins broke when we turned on SameSite and CSRF protection", level:4, domains:["p31","p5"],
  context:"You maintain the customer portal for a property-management company: a Node.js/Express app at portal.example.com behind nginx, which terminates TLS and proxies to the app over plain HTTP. Release 3.8.0 went out at 06:00 with the hardening from last month's application security review: Secure, HttpOnly and SameSite on the session cookie, a __Host- cookie name, an Origin check on state-changing requests, and a synchronizer CSRF token. QA passed on developer laptops at http://localhost:3000. Since 06:00, password logins fail with 403, and single sign-on through the company IdP (idp.example.net, OIDC) bounces users back to the login page. A teammate has opened a hotfix PR. You must restore logins today without weakening the protection the review asked for.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Release 3.8.0 (app.js excerpt) and the nginx site",
     text:"// app.js (3.8.0)\nconst prod = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production';\napp.use(session({\n  name: prod ? '__Host-sid' : 'sid',\n  secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET, resave: false, saveUninitialized: false,\n  store: new RedisStore({ client: redis }),\n  cookie: { secure: prod, httpOnly: true, sameSite: 'strict', path: '/', maxAge: 8 * 3600 * 1000 }\n}));\napp.use(function originCheck(req, res, next) {\n  if (['GET', 'HEAD', 'OPTIONS'].includes(req.method)) return next();\n  const expected = `${req.protocol}://${req.get('host')}`;\n  if (req.get('origin') !== expected) {\n    log.warn({ origin: req.get('origin'), expected }, 'CSRF origin mismatch');\n    return res.status(403).send('Forbidden');\n  }\n  next();\n});\napp.use(csrfSynchronisedProtection);   // csrf-sync: token kept in req.session\napp.listen(3000);                      // no host argument\n# /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/portal (location block)\n    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;\n    proxy_set_header Host $host;\n    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;"},
    {label:"Logs, Monday 06:05-07:30",
     text:"app (pino):\n{\"level\":40,\"time\":\"2026-10-26T06:12:44.118Z\",\"origin\":\"https://portal.example.com\",\"expected\":\"http://portal.example.com\",\"msg\":\"CSRF origin mismatch\"}\n{\"level\":50,\"time\":\"2026-10-26T06:14:02.507Z\",\"err\":\"did not find expected authorization request details in session, req.session[\\\"oidc:idp.example.net\\\"] is undefined\",\"msg\":\"oidc callback failed\"}\napp, staging replay with DEBUG=express-session:\n  express-session not secured\n  express-session saving lKz2o3Nq...\nnginx access.log:\n203.0.113.24 - - [26/Oct/2026:06:12:31 +0000] \"GET /login HTTP/2.0\" 200 4110 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0 ...\"\n203.0.113.24 - - [26/Oct/2026:06:12:44 +0000] \"POST /login HTTP/2.0\" 403 9 \"https://portal.example.com/login\" \"Mozilla/5.0 ...\"\n198.51.100.7 - - [26/Oct/2026:06:14:02 +0000] \"GET /auth/callback?code=...&state=... HTTP/2.0\" 302 46 \"https://idp.example.net/\" \"Mozilla/5.0 ...\"\nnginx error.log:\n2026/10/26 06:20:31 [warn] 2211#2211: *8812 an upstream response is buffered to a temporary file\n  /var/lib/nginx/proxy/3/07/0000000073 while reading upstream, request: \"GET /reports/2026-09.pdf HTTP/2.0\""},
    {label:"Staging experiment, browser DevTools and the production host",
     text:"Staging, after adding app.set('trust proxy', 'loopback') and nginx 'proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;':\n  password login: 302 -> /dashboard (works)\n  SSO: still fails with the same 'req.session[\"oidc:idp.example.net\"] is undefined'\nDevTools, staging SSO attempt:\n  GET /auth/login  response header:\n    Set-Cookie: __Host-sid=s%3AkP9...; Path=/; Expires=Mon, 26 Oct 2026 16:41:10 GMT; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict\n  GET /auth/callback?code=...&state=...  (navigation initiated by idp.example.net)\n    Request headers: Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site; Sec-Fetch-Mode: navigate; no Cookie header\nProduction host:\n$ grep -rn \"trust proxy\" src/ || echo \"no matches\"\nno matches\n$ ss -tlnp | grep 3000\nLISTEN 0  511  *:3000  *:*  users:((\"node\",pid=2381,fd=21))\n$ ip -br addr show ens192\nens192  UP  10.30.4.21/24"},
    {label:"Teammate's hotfix, PR #388",
     text:"-  cookie: { secure: prod, httpOnly: true, sameSite: 'strict', path: '/', maxAge: 8 * 3600 * 1000 }\n+  cookie: { secure: false, httpOnly: true, sameSite: 'none', path: '/', maxAge: 8 * 3600 * 1000 }\n-app.use(function originCheck(req, res, next) {\n+app.use(['/api', '/account'], function originCheck(req, res, next) {   // skip /login and /auth\n+app.set('trust proxy', true);\nPR description: 'Unblocks logins. We can re-harden next sprint.'"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp31q1", q:"Why does the password login fail with expected 'http://portal.example.com'?",
     options:["SameSite=Strict stops the browser from sending the cookie on a same-site POST","nginx strips the Origin header before proxying","The app sees the nginx-to-Node hop as plain HTTP. nginx doesn't send X-Forwarded-Proto and Express doesn't trust the proxy, so req.protocol is 'http'. The Origin check compares against the wrong scheme, and express-session refuses to set a Secure cookie on what it thinks is an insecure connection ('not secured'), so the CSRF token never reaches a session either","The __Host- prefix requires a Domain attribute, which is missing"],
     answer:2, why:"The log shows the browser's real Origin (https) against an expected value built from req.protocol (http). Strict cookies are sent on same-site requests, and this POST is same-site. The Origin header arrived intact, since the log prints it. __Host- forbids a Domain attribute; it doesn't require one."},
    {id:"fcp31q2", q:"With trust proxy and X-Forwarded-Proto fixed in staging, password login works but SSO still fails. Why?",
     options:["The IdP's clock is skewed, so the ID token is rejected","The session cookie is SameSite=Strict. The callback navigation is initiated by idp.example.net, a different site, so the browser withholds Strict cookies and the app can't find the stored state. A SameSite=Lax cookie is sent on a top-level GET navigation, which is what the callback is","The state parameter is being URL-encoded twice","PKCE fails because the code_verifier is too short"],
     answer:1, why:"DevTools shows the callback request with Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site and no Cookie header, while the cookie itself was set correctly with Secure and Strict. The error is 'session details undefined', which comes before any token or PKCE check. Lax still blocks cookies on cross-site POSTs and subresource requests, so it keeps the CSRF protection that matters."},
    {id:"fcp31q3", q:"Should PR #388 be merged?",
     options:["Yes, as a temporary unblock, with re-hardening next sprint","Yes, but keep sameSite 'none'","Only the trust proxy line","No. It wouldn't even work, because browsers reject a __Host- cookie without Secure, and a SameSite=None cookie without Secure. It also removes CSRF protection from login and SSO, and trust proxy true believes forwarded headers from anyone who can reach port 3000, which is listening on 10.30.4.21"],
     answer:3, why:"The PR weakens three protections and still leaves logins broken. 'trust proxy true' trusts every hop's X-Forwarded-* headers. With Node listening on all interfaces, any LAN host can send X-Forwarded-Proto: https over plain HTTP and be treated as secure. Login CSRF (forcing a victim into an attacker-chosen session) is exactly what an Origin check on /login prevents."},
    {id:"fcp31q4", q:"Which configuration restores logins and keeps the protection?",
     options:["nginx sets proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme (overwriting any client-supplied value); Express trusts only loopback and listens on 127.0.0.1; the session cookie stays __Host-, Secure and HttpOnly with SameSite=Lax; the Origin check and CSRF token stay on every state-changing route","Set trust proxy to true and leave Node on all interfaces, since nginx is the only intended client","Drop the Origin check and rely on SameSite=Lax alone","Set SameSite=None; Secure on the session cookie so SSO works everywhere"],
     answer:0, why:"The app then learns the real scheme only from the one proxy it trusts, which nobody else can reach. The cookie keeps every protective attribute, and Lax is the strictest setting that survives the OIDC redirect. Dropping the Origin check removes a layer the review asked for, and SameSite=None gives up the CSRF protection of SameSite entirely."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the fix and review note. Give the root cause of each failure with evidence, why QA didn't catch it, the exact nginx and Express changes, why the hotfix PR must be rejected, and the tests that prove logins work and the protections still hold.",
    rubric:["Traces the password-login failure to the TLS-terminating proxy: no X-Forwarded-Proto and no trust proxy, so req.protocol is 'http', the Origin check expects http://, and express-session logs 'not secured' and never sets the Secure cookie.","Explains the SSO failure separately: SameSite=Strict withholds the session cookie on the cross-site-initiated callback (no Cookie header, Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site), so the OIDC state is missing. Lax fixes it while still blocking cross-site POSTs.","Specifies the fix: nginx overwrites X-Forwarded-Proto with $scheme, Express trusts only loopback, Node binds to 127.0.0.1 (or a host firewall blocks 3000), and the cookie stays __Host-, Secure and HttpOnly with SameSite=Lax.","Rejects PR #388 with reasons: __Host- and SameSite=None cookies require Secure, so it wouldn't work; it removes CSRF checks from login and SSO; and trust proxy true with port 3000 exposed lets anyone fake forwarded headers.","Explains why QA missed it (HTTP on localhost, NODE_ENV not production, no proxy, no cross-site IdP) and adds a staging environment behind the real nginx with TLS and the real IdP.","Verifies: password and SSO logins succeed, a cross-site form POST and a POST with a bad Origin or missing token get 403, cookie attributes are correct, and a direct request to port 3000 from the LAN fails. Dismisses the nginx buffering warning."],
    reference:"Both failures come from the proxy hop plus one cookie setting. nginx terminates TLS and talks to Node over plain HTTP. It doesn't send X-Forwarded-Proto, and the app never sets trust proxy, so Express sees req.protocol as 'http' and req.secure as false. The Origin check therefore expects 'http://portal.example.com' and rejects the browser's genuine https Origin. express-session also refuses to set a Secure cookie on what it thinks is an insecure connection ('not secured'), so no session cookie, and no CSRF token, ever reaches the browser. QA ran on http://localhost with secure off and no proxy, so none of this could show up. The staging test proves a second cause. With the proxy fixed, password login works but SSO still fails. The callback navigation is started by idp.example.net, a different site, so the browser withholds the SameSite=Strict cookie (no Cookie header, Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site) and the stored OIDC state is missing. Lax is sent on top-level GET navigations and still blocks cross-site POSTs. Fix: in nginx, proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme. In Express, app.set('trust proxy', 'loopback') and app.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1'). Keep __Host-sid with Secure and HttpOnly, change sameSite to 'lax', and keep the Origin check and CSRF tokens. Reject PR #388: browsers drop __Host- and SameSite=None cookies without Secure, it strips CSRF protection from login, and trust proxy true with port 3000 open lets anyone fake the scheme. Verify both login paths, a forged cross-site POST (403), the cookie attributes, and that port 3000 is unreachable from the LAN.",
    alternatives:["Adding https://portal.example.com to an allowed-origins list makes the Origin check pass, but req.secure is still false, so express-session still won't set the Secure cookie. It treats one symptom.","Trusting the proxy's exact address (app.set('trust proxy', '127.0.0.1')) instead of 'loopback' is equivalent. A hop count (trust proxy 1) also works if nginx is always exactly one hop and the app port isn't reachable.","Keeping the session cookie Strict and adding a separate short-lived, SameSite=Lax state cookie scoped to /auth is acceptable and slightly stricter, at the cost of more code.","Switching the IdP to response_mode=form_post makes the callback a cross-site POST, which even Lax cookies don't accompany. That would need a SameSite=None; Secure state cookie, so avoid it unless the IdP requires it."] },
  hints:["Compare the 'origin' and 'expected' values in the app log. Where does Express get the scheme from?","What headers does nginx send upstream, and what does express-session do with a Secure cookie on a connection it thinks is HTTP? For SSO, look at who starts the callback navigation and at the cookie's SameSite value.","Add X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme in nginx, trust only loopback, bind Node to 127.0.0.1, and change SameSite from Strict to Lax. Keep Secure, __Host-, the Origin check and CSRF tokens, and reject the hotfix."] },

{ id:"fcp32", kind:"case", title:"A threat feed that blocked the payroll site", level:4, domains:["p32"],
  context:"You are the one-person threat intelligence function at a regional credit union with 400 staff. A self-hosted MISP instance aggregates eleven feeds, and every 15 minutes a script exports its IP indicators to the perimeter firewall as an external dynamic list, which a deny rule called TI-AUTO-BLOCK enforces. At 08:05 on Monday the help desk lit up: the payroll provider, the core-banking vendor's support portal, a document-signing service and dozens of other sites fail for most staff, and branch tellers can't reach the card processor's status page. The CISO wants service back within the hour without simply switching the feed off, and then a fix to the indicator pipeline so this can't happen again.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Firewall deny log, 08:00-08:30 (summary and sample)",
     text:"rule=TI-AUTO-BLOCK  denies 08:00-08:30: 41,882   distinct internal sources: 361\ntop destinations:\n  203.0.113.44:443    37,915 denies   SNI seen: payroll.example.com, sign.example.net, help.example.org (+211 more)\n  198.51.100.80:443    2,906 denies   SNI seen: status.example.com, cdn.example.org (+47 more)\n  192.0.2.17:443         641 denies   SNI seen: updates.example.net\n  (19 other IPs)         420 denies\nsample:\n2026-10-26T08:03:12 deny TI-AUTO-BLOCK src=10.40.12.55 dst=203.0.113.44:443 sni=payroll.example.com list=misp-ip-block\n2026-10-26T08:03:15 deny TI-AUTO-BLOCK src=10.40.18.20 dst=203.0.113.44:443 sni=sign.example.net list=misp-ip-block\n2026-10-26T08:04:02 deny TI-AUTO-BLOCK src=10.52.3.11 dst=198.51.100.80:443 sni=status.example.com list=misp-ip-block\nEDL refresh 06:00: 61,480 entries, refresh took 38 s, management-plane CPU 81% during refresh"},
    {label:"MISP: the attribute behind 203.0.113.44",
     text:"Attribute 203.0.113.44   type: ip-dst   category: Network activity   to_ids: true\nEvent #18822 'Community OSINT feed 7 - daily batch 2026-03-02'\n  Source org: feed-osint7    Published: 2026-10-25 21:40 (re-published by feed sync)\n  Event date: 2026-03-02   first_seen: 2026-03-02   last_seen: 2026-03-04\n  Tags: tlp:clear, admiralty-scale:source-reliability=\"f\",\n        estimative-language:confidence-in-analytic-judgment=\"low\"\n  Comment: 'auto-extracted from a public URL list'\nWarninglist hit: 'List of known IPv4 public CDN ranges' (203.0.113.0/24)\nCorrelations: 0    Sightings: none recorded\nDecay model: none applied\nSTIX export: \"confidence\": 15, \"valid_from\": \"2026-03-02T00:00:00Z\" (no valid_until)\nPassive DNS, last 30 days: 2,340 distinct domains resolved to 203.0.113.44"},
    {label:"The export job and the list's history",
     text:"# /opt/ti/export_edl.py (excerpt)\nbody = {\"returnFormat\": \"text\", \"type\": \"ip-dst\", \"to_ids\": 1, \"published\": 1,\n        \"enforceWarninglist\": False}          # no age, tag or confidence filter\nr = s.post(MISP + \"/attributes/restSearch\", json=body, timeout=60)\nwrite_edl(\"/var/www/edl/misp-ip-block.txt\", r.text)   # firewall pulls it every 15 min\n\nList size: Jan 2,104 | Apr 18,770 | Jul 39,215 | Oct 25 47,912 | Oct 26 61,480\nEntries ever removed by expiry: 0\nOct 25 21:40: feed-osint7 re-published 412 old events (13,568 ip-dst attributes,\n  event dates Feb-Apr 2026)\nOf today's 61,480: on a MISP warninglist 2,911 | last_seen over 90 days ago 44,307 |\n  source reliability E or F 29,840\nSOC review of the last 30 days: 1,903 distinct blocked IPs matched our traffic;\n  1,862 were on CDN or cloud warninglists; 4 confirmed true positives; 37 undetermined"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp32q1", q:"Why does one indicator, 203.0.113.44, break hundreds of unrelated sites?",
     options:["It's a shared CDN edge address that serves thousands of domains (2,340 in passive DNS, more than 200 in our own SNI logs). Blocking the IP blocks every site behind it, which is the collateral cost of IP indicators on shared infrastructure","The payroll provider and the other vendors were all compromised at the same time","DNS poisoning pointed those names at a malicious address","The firewall's list parser corrupted the entries"],
     answer:0, why:"An IP address identifies infrastructure, and on a CDN or cloud edge that infrastructure is shared by thousands of tenants. IPs sit near the bottom of the Pyramid of Pain because they are cheap to change and, as here, they collide with legitimate traffic. The warninglist hit flagged exactly this. Nothing in the evidence suggests every vendor was compromised or that DNS changed. The same names resolve to their usual CDN address."},
    {id:"fcp32q2", q:"The indicator dates from March. Why did it start blocking on Monday?",
     options:["The firewall only refreshes the list once a week","MISP correlated it with a new event over the weekend","The feed re-published 412 old events on Sunday night, and the export takes every published to_ids attribute with no age, confidence or warninglist filter and no expiry. A seven-month-old, low-confidence indicator went straight into the block list","The CDN moved the payroll site onto a malicious address on Monday"],
     answer:2, why:"The event's published time is 2026-10-25 21:40, while first_seen and last_seen are in early March. The script filters only on type, to_ids and published, with enforceWarninglist off, and nothing has ever expired from the list. The list refreshes every 15 minutes, and the attribute has zero correlations."},
    {id:"fcp32q3", q:"Over the last 30 days, 1,903 blocked IPs matched our traffic, with 4 confirmed true positives and 37 undetermined. What does that say about the block list?",
     options:["It's working well: it stopped four real threats","At best about 2% of its blocks are justified (41 of 1,903, even if every undetermined case is real), so at least 98% are false positives. For these sources, blocking on IPs costs more than it protects, and they belong in alerting until they meet a confidence and freshness bar","False positives are acceptable because blocking fails safe","The numbers are too small to draw any conclusion"],
     answer:1, why:"Precision is what matters for an automated block. 4 to 41 good blocks against more than 1,800 that interrupted business is a measured false-positive rate, not a guess, and 1,862 of the hits were on warninglisted ranges the export could have excluded. 'Fails safe' ignores that blocking payroll and card-status pages is itself an availability incident."},
    {id:"fcp32q4", q:"Which change fixes the pipeline without throwing away the intelligence?",
     options:["Disable the feed and the dynamic list permanently","Block whole /24 networks instead of single IPs to keep up with infrastructure changes","Review all 61,480 indicators by hand","Send to the block list only indicators that pass enforceWarninglist, a source-reliability and confidence threshold, and a type-specific lifetime (for example, IPs last seen within 30 days, via a MISP decay model). Everything else goes to an alert-only list for the SIEM, with an allowlist for critical SaaS and a review gate when a refresh changes the list by more than a set amount"],
     answer:3, why:"Indicator quality and lifecycle are the controls: warninglists, source grading, confidence and expiry decide what is strong enough to block automatically, and weaker indicators still feed detection. The change-size gate would have caught a 13,568-entry jump. Blocking /24s multiplies collateral damage, and hand review of 61,480 entries doesn't scale and doesn't stop the next bad batch."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the incident note and the new indicator policy. Explain what caused the outage with evidence, give the immediate steps in order to restore service while keeping protection, set out the export and lifecycle rules you'll put in place, and say how you'll measure feed quality from now on.",
    rubric:["Explains why one IP took down hundreds of sites: shared CDN infrastructure (2,340 domains in passive DNS, more than 200 SNI names in our logs) and the collateral cost of IP indicators.","Traces how it got in: the feed re-published 412 old events (13,568 IPs), and the export takes every published to_ids attribute with enforceWarninglist off, no age, confidence or source filter, and no expiry (0 entries ever expired).","Restores service fast without switching everything off: an exception list above the deny rule for the CDN addresses, or a re-export with warninglists enforced and the re-published batch excluded, then a forced list refresh and a check that payroll and the other sites load.","Defines lifecycle and quality rules: warninglists enforced, a minimum source reliability and confidence for blocking, type-specific lifetimes or a MISP decay model, separate block and alert lists, and a change-size gate on each refresh.","Quantifies quality from the 30-day review (at most 41 of 1,903, about 2%, justified) and sets ongoing metrics: false-positive rate per feed, age at delivery, overlap between feeds, and sightings recorded back in MISP.","Dismisses the 81% management-plane CPU during the refresh as a capacity note, not the cause."],
    reference:"At 21:40 on Sunday, community feed 7 re-published 412 events from February to April, 13,568 IP indicators. Our export pulls every published to_ids ip-dst attribute with enforceWarninglist off and no age, confidence or source filter, and nothing has ever expired from the list (2,104 entries in January, 61,480 today). One of those indicators was 203.0.113.44, a shared CDN edge address with 2,340 domains in passive DNS and over 200 in our own SNI logs, including payroll and the signing service. Blocking an IP blocks everything behind it, and this one was rated reliability F, low confidence, last seen in March, and on a CDN warninglist. The 81% management CPU during the refresh is a capacity note, not the cause. Within the hour: add an exception list above TI-AUTO-BLOCK for the warninglisted CDN addresses, rerun the export with enforceWarninglist on and the re-published batch excluded, force a list refresh, and confirm payroll and the card-status page load. New policy: only indicators that clear warninglists, source reliability A to C, a confidence threshold and a lifetime (IPs last seen within 30 days, via a MISP decay model) go to the block list. Everything else goes to an alert-only list for the SIEM. Critical SaaS goes on an allowlist, and a refresh that changes the list by more than 5% waits for review. Quality: the last 30 days justified at most 41 of 1,903 blocks, about 2%. Track false positives per feed, age at delivery and overlap monthly, and record sightings in MISP.",
    alternatives:["Moving all IP indicators to alert-only and blocking only on high-confidence domains and URLs is defensible and cuts collateral damage, but domain indicators need lifetimes and warninglists too, because shared hosting and URL shorteners collide in the same way.","Removing feed-osint7 entirely ends today's outage, but the export still has no expiry or warninglist filter, so the next feed that re-publishes old data does the same thing.","Blocking whole /24 ranges makes collateral damage worse, not better: it would cut off every tenant in that CDN range."] },
  hints:["Count the different SNI names behind the top blocked IP, and check what passive DNS says about that address.","When was the indicator first and last seen, when was it published, and what does the export script filter on?","A re-published, seven-month-old, low-confidence CDN address went straight to a block list with no warninglist, age filter or expiry. Add an exception for the CDN addresses now, enforce warninglists, add confidence and lifetime rules, and move weak IPs to alert-only."] },

{ id:"fcp33", kind:"case", title:"SOC 2 fieldwork: backups without restores, reviews with gaps", level:4, domains:["p33"],
  context:"You run governance, risk and compliance for a 120-person SaaS company that sells scheduling and billing software to clinics. Its first SOC 2 Type II report covers 1 January to 30 September 2026 and is due to customers on 15 December. Two hospital customers have made a clean report a condition of renewal. Last week the audit firm sent its draft exceptions from fieldwork. The CEO asks whether you can 'get these closed before the report goes out' and wants a remediation plan with owners and dates by Friday. You own the control library, the evidence repository and management's responses.",
  evidence:[
    {label:"Auditor's draft exceptions (excerpt)",
     text:"Exception 1 - Control A1.3-02: 'Production databases are backed up daily and restore\n  tests are performed quarterly.'\n  Condition: backup job logs inspected for 25 sampled days: 25 of 25 successful.\n  No evidence of a restore test in Q1, Q2 or Q3 2026 was provided.\n  Criteria: TSC A1.3 (recovery plan testing), CC7.5 (recovery activities)\nException 2 - Control CC6.2-04: 'Privileged access to production (AWS, database) is reviewed\n  quarterly by the system owner; inappropriate access is removed within 5 business days.'\n  Condition: Q1 review provided, signed and dated 2026-04-08. Q2: spreadsheet provided,\n  undated, no reviewer sign-off. Q3: review dated 2026-09-24; population taken from the\n  HR roster; the reviewer approved her own administrator access.\nObservation (not an exception): vulnerability scan shows 3 critical findings on host sandbox-07.\nSystem description boundary: production AWS account and production database; the sandbox\n  AWS account is outside the system boundary."},
    {label:"Backup and recovery evidence",
     text:"Backup console, 2026-01-01 to 2026-09-30:\n  prod-pg-01 nightly snapshot + WAL archive: 273 of 273 jobs successful, copied to a second region\n  Restore tests logged: none\nINC-2291 (2026-08-14): accidental table drop in the billing schema\n  Restore started 09:12; database available 04:22 the next day: 19 h 10 min\n  Notes: 'runbook referenced the old bucket name; WAL replay slower than expected;\n  no one on the team had done a restore before'\nBusiness continuity plan v2.1 (approved 2026-01-15):\n  scheduling and billing RTO 4 hours, RPO 15 minutes\nHospital A contract, section 9.2: 'Provider shall maintain an RTO of no more than 8 hours.'"},
    {label:"Access review artifacts and the reconciliation you ran this week",
     text:"Q3 review sheet (from the HR roster, 2026-09-24): 38 people listed, 38 marked 'keep'\n  Reviewer: M. Ortiz, engineering manager\nReconciliation 2026-10-21: AWS IAM Identity Center admin permission sets and database\n  superuser roles compared with the Q3 sheet\n  identities with production admin today:                                  47\n  on the Q3 sheet:                                                         38\n  not on the sheet: service accounts (ci-deploy, backup-agent, dbt-runner)  3\n                    contractors provisioned directly in AWS, not in HR      4\n                    former employees (terminated 2026-06-12, 2026-07-30)    2\n  former employees' sign-ins after termination (CloudTrail): none\n  M. Ortiz's own admin access: approved by M. Ortiz\nQ2 artifact: access_review_Q2.xlsx, created 2026-07-03 per file metadata,\n  no reviewer field, no tickets for any removals\nCEO, by email: 'Can we just do the Q2 review now and date it June 30?'"}
  ],
  questions:[
    {id:"fcp33q1", q:"The auditor didn't accept 25 of 25 successful backup jobs as evidence for Exception 1. Why?",
     options:["The logs should have been screenshots with visible timestamps","25 sampled days is too small a sample","Successful jobs prove a copy was written, not that it can be restored within the recovery objectives. The control promises quarterly restore tests, and nothing shows one happened","Backups only count if they are encrypted"],
     answer:2, why:"Evidence has to prove the control as written. The control has two parts, daily backups and quarterly restore tests, and only the first was evidenced. A dated restore-test ticket with measured time and verified data is the standard proof. Screenshots are weaker evidence than system logs, and 25 is a normal sample for a daily control."},
    {id:"fcp33q2", q:"What does INC-2291 mean for this report?",
     options:["It closes Exception 1, because a restore did happen in Q3","Nothing, because incidents are outside the audit's scope","Leave it out of the evidence package","It shows the restore capability doesn't meet the plan: 19 h 10 min against a 4-hour RTO, and beyond the contract's 8 hours. It doesn't satisfy the quarterly test control, it supports the auditor's concern, and it must be disclosed honestly. The remedy is to make restores meet 4 hours, or to change the RTO through a business impact analysis the business owner signs, within the contract's 8 hours"],
     answer:3, why:"An unplanned restore under pressure is evidence of capability, and here it fails both the internal objective and a contractual commitment. Hiding it would be a serious integrity failure, and incidents in the period are in scope for CC7.5. Whether the answer is engineering (faster restores) or governance (a different RTO) is a business decision, but the contract sets the ceiling."},
    {id:"fcp33q3", q:"The CEO asks you to do the Q2 review now and date it 30 June. What do you do?",
     options:["Do it, since the access was the same in June","Refuse. Backdating evidence is falsification and would undermine the whole report. The Q2 exception stands for this period. Do a complete review now (from the systems of record, with an independent reviewer), remove inappropriate access, and record it in management's response and as evidence for the next period","Ask the auditor to drop Q2 from the sample","Re-scope the report to exclude privileged access"],
     answer:1, why:"A SOC 2 Type II report describes what actually operated during the period. The file metadata already shows the Q2 sheet was created on 3 July, and fabricated evidence turns one exception into a fraud problem that can void the report. Auditors choose their samples, and re-scoping after fieldwork to hide a failing control isn't credible. Remediate now and prove it in the next period."},
    {id:"fcp33q4", q:"What made the Q3 review deficient even though it was signed and dated inside the quarter?",
     options:["The population came from HR, not from the systems that grant access, so 9 of 47 admin identities (service accounts, directly provisioned contractors and two former employees) were never reviewed. And the reviewer approved her own access, so the review wasn't independent","It used a spreadsheet instead of a GRC platform","It should have been monthly, not quarterly","It carried an electronic signature rather than a wet one"],
     answer:0, why:"Good evidence is complete: the population must come from the system of record (IAM and the database), or the review can't see what HR doesn't know about. Self-approval defeats segregation of duties. The tool and the signature type don't matter, and the control says quarterly."}
  ],
  task:{ prompt:"Write the remediation plan and draft management responses for both exceptions. Cover the cause of each gap, what you do immediately, the control and evidence redesign (population, frequency, reviewer, artifacts), owners and dates, and what you'll tell the CEO and the two hospital customers about this report and the next.",
    rubric:["States that exceptions in the 1 January to 30 September period can't be fixed retroactively, refuses to backdate the Q2 review, and plans management responses that describe the remediation honestly.","Backups: explains why job success isn't restore evidence, schedules an immediate full restore test of prod-pg-01 with measured time and verified data, sets a quarterly cadence with tickets as evidence, and fixes the runbook defects INC-2291 exposed.","Reconciles the 19 h 10 min restore with the 4-hour RTO and the contract's 8 hours: either engineer restores to meet the RTO or revise it through a signed BIA, staying within the contract.","Access review: rebuilds the population from the systems of record (47 identities), removes the two former employees at once, justifies or removes the contractor and service accounts within 5 business days, uses an independent reviewer for the reviewer's own access, and fixes the leaver process.","Defines evidence for the next period: system-generated exports, dated sign-offs inside each quarter, tickets for every removal, and automated monitoring of admin membership against HR and the IdP.","Treats the sandbox-07 findings as outside the system boundary for this report but routes them to normal vulnerability management, and tells customers the truth: the exceptions, the remediation and when the evidence will appear."],
    reference:"Neither exception can be closed before the report goes out: the period ended on 30 September, and backdating the Q2 review would be falsifying evidence. The plan is to remediate now, prove it in the next period, and say so in management's responses. Exception 1: 273 successful jobs prove copies were written, not that they can be restored. The only real restore, INC-2291, took 19 h 10 min against a 4-hour RTO because the runbook was stale and nobody had practiced. Actions: the platform lead fixes the runbook by 6 November; a full restore of prod-pg-01 into an isolated environment by 13 November, timed and verified with row counts and a smoke test; quarterly tests after that, with tickets as evidence. If 4 hours can't be met, the business owner revises the RTO through a BIA, within Hospital A's 8-hour contract. Exception 2: the Q3 review came from the HR roster, so 9 of 47 admin identities were never reviewed, including two former employees (CloudTrail shows no sign-ins after termination), and the reviewer approved her own access. Actions: remove the leavers today; justify or remove the four contractors and three service accounts within 5 business days; redo the review from IAM and database exports, with the CTO reviewing the reviewer's access; alert on admin membership that doesn't match HR and the IdP. The sandbox-07 findings are outside the boundary and go to normal vulnerability management. Tell the hospitals plainly: two exceptions, remediation under way, evidence in the next report.",
    alternatives:["Asking the audit firm for a separate point-in-time report on the remediated controls can reassure customers sooner, but it doesn't change the Type II report's exceptions.","Weakening the control's promise for future periods (for example, annual restore tests) is legitimate only if the risk assessment supports it. Rewording it now to match what happened is not.","Buying a GRC automation platform helps collect evidence continuously, but it wouldn't have caught a review population drawn from the wrong system unless its integrations read IAM and the database rather than HR."] },
  hints:["For each control, compare what it promises with what the evidence actually proves. What did the auditor sample?","Compare INC-2291's restore time with the BCP and the contract, and compare the Q3 review's population with your reconciliation.","You can't fix the past period, and you don't backdate. Restore-test now and quarterly (and settle the 19-hour vs 4-hour gap), rebuild the access review from systems of record with an independent reviewer, remove the leavers, and write honest management responses."] }
);
